{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .agents/skills/pose-review/SKILL.md; cat POSE.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .agents/skills/pose-review/SKILL.md; cat POSE.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: pose-review\ndescription: Use for POSE pull-request or code review to verify controlled scope, preserved contracts, security and observability impact, risk-proportional validation, and escalation where applicable. Trigger keywords - review, code review, PR review, review opinion, ultrareview.\nwhen_to_use: Evaluating your own or another author's diff or PR under POSE. Use before commenting or approving to select rules, inspect validation evidence and prior decisions, and issue an actionable decision.\npose_schema_range: \"1-1\"\nclients: agents-skills, mcp, claude-code\ncapabilities: read\n---\n\n# Skill: pose-review\n\n## Required reading\n\n1. [AGENTS.md](../../../AGENTS.md).\n2. [`.pose/workflows/review.md`](../../../.pose/workflows/review.md).\n3. Applicable domain rules; security takes precedence in conflicts.\n\nResolve the selected task with `pose context --task <typed-or-qualified-artifact-ref> --json` before reviewing. Follow its canonical authority and stop on ambiguity, stale binding, unsupported metadata or `transfer-in-progress`. Use the qualified `xref:` scope for cross-project review; every cross-project write requires an explicit `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` binding and the current `context_revision` as `--expect-context`. Keep review evidence and its `POSE-Spec:` trailer in the authority repository.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Classify the change as feature, bugfix, refactor, documentation, or mixed.\n2. Run `pose assess tech-debt` during code review to ensure markers (`TODO`, `FIXME`, `stub`, `panic`) are covered by follow-ups or specs. Run `pose assess integrate` when touching inter-module contracts (Protobuf, Kafka, REST, MCP).\n3. Resolve `pose review bundle <scope> --explain` to inspect effective criteria, components and tool requirements.\n4. Run active required tools first, record why each recommended tool was used or skipped, and keep completion tools deferred until attestation.\n5. Select rules with `pose suggest review --path <affected-dir>` for every mapped component.\n6. Search `.pose/knowledge/` for prior module decisions, accepted risks, and pending follow-ups.\n7. Require deterministic `pose validate --strict` evidence matching delivery targets.\n8. Evaluate every required plan criterion, including cross-component boundaries. When the change adds or alters a governance gate, also answer: what is the cheapest way to satisfy this gate in form without producing its value, and what stops that? Point to the adversarial corpus case that proves it (`pose-mcp/internal/cli/testdata/adversarial/README.md`), or record the gap.\n9. Classify findings as critical, high, medium, or low with evidence and expected action.\n10. Run `pose recurrence-check --tolerant --window-days 14`; use recurrence escalation for a matching systemic signal.\n11. Create a handoff with `pose new-knowledge handoff <slug>` for accepted residual risk, monitoring, or deferred action.\n12. Seal the review subject with `pose review bundle <scope> --seal`.\n13. Prepare the mechanical half with `pose review auto-attest <bundle-id> --reviewer agent:<id>` — without `--apply` — and read `review_attestation.pending`: those are the criteria no registered check answers, and they are yours to answer. Then record the attestation with `pose review attest`, giving every judgment criterion a conclusion. `--apply` on auto-attest records only when nothing is pending; it refuses otherwise, and never answers a judgment criterion for you. An explicit attestation records `--criterion ID|passed|evidence` (or `not-applicable` with a rationale, or `finding` naming a recorded finding) and `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]`; every finding needs a severity and an action, and an `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` finding also needs an owner, a rationale and a review date. A `passed` criterion may cite only evidence the sealed bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts. For a cross-project attestation, use the qualified `xref:` as the target argument to `pose review attest`, include `--expect-context <digest>` with `--apply`, and keep the explicit project binding; a bundle ID alone does not select an authority project.\n14. Run `pose review verify <scope>` and `pose review-check <scope>`; close only when both confirm a valid, approved attestation.\n15. Decide: approved, approved with reservations, changes requested, or rejected.\n16. When Contributor Mode is active, if review uncovers POSE linter false-positives, diagnostic frictions, or rule gaps, stage an improvement proposal with `pose contribute stage --type enhancement --title \"<summary>\"`.\n\n## Output requirements\n\n- Completed Rules applied during review section.\n- Bundle/plan digests and dispositions for required, recommended and deferred completion tools.\n- Severity-classified findings with expected actions.\n- Clear and actionable final decision.\n- Handoff when residual risk is accepted.\n# POSE — Project Operating Standard for Engineering\n\n## 1) What it is\n\nPOSE is the operating standard for agent work in **{{PROJECT_NAME}}**.\n\nPrimary goals:\n\n- reduce ambiguity in tasks\n- improve execution predictability\n- make validation and reporting consistent\n- scale collaboration in a heterogeneous repository\n\nPOSE does **not** replace product architecture or existing security policies;\nit organizes how agents execute technical work.\n\nThe short agent contract lives in [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md); this document is\nthe operating manual (structure, CLI, per-type flows, CI, governance).\n\n---\n\n## 2) Principles\n\n1. **Scope first**: read only the instructions and artifacts needed for the affected directories.\n2. **Plan before implementing**: non-trivial changes go through a spec/plan.\n3. **Incrementalism**: small, cohesive, auditable deliveries.\n4. **Deterministic validation**: prefer reproducible commands (`test`, `lint`, `typecheck`, `build`, contract/security checks).\n5. **Risk transparency**: always surface gaps and human-review points.\n\n---\n\n## 3) Structure\n\n```text\n.pose/\n  workflows/     # procedure per work type\n  templates/     # spec.md, roadmap.md, knowledge.md, changelog-fragment.md, doc-audit-report.md\n  rules/         # domain rules (cumulative)\n  knowledge/     # handoffs and notes with active governance\n  adr/           # architectural decisions\n  roadmaps/      # governed roadmaps (milestone DAGs)\n  changelogs/    # pending fragments plus immutable version archives/notes\n  releases/      # version manifests and append-only lifecycle evidence\n  indexes/       # repo-map, services, packages, validation-matrix, module-metadata, task-map, spec-graph, roadmaps\n  reports/       # versionable reports + history JSONL + archive/\n  specs/         # living specs per feature\n  schema-version # instance contract version (see `pose update`)\n\n.agents/skills/  # skills (source of truth; Codex-native format)\n.claude/skills/  # Claude Code-compatible symlinks\npose             # native Go binary available on PATH\nAGENTS.md        # short operating contract\nPOSE.md          # this manual\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4) Key files\n\n- [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md): short contract, precedence and entry points.\n- Subproject `AGENTS.md` (when present): local guidance, applied only to that directory's scope.\n- [`.pose/workflows/*.md`](.pose/workflows/): procedure per work type (`feature`, `bugfix`, `review`, `refactor`, `documentation-update`, `recurrence-escalation`).\n- [`.pose/rules/*.md`](.pose/rules/): domain rules; recurring content lives in [`.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md`](.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md).\n- [`.pose/templates/spec.md`](.pose/templates/spec.md): the single per-feature spec template.\n- `.pose/project.json`: the project's declared identity, `{\"schema_version\":1,\"project_id\":\"proj.<name>\",\"name\":\"…\"}`, written by `pose install` and seeded by `pose update` from the id an older instance declared in `.mcp.json` or `AGENTS.md`. Resolution reads `POSE_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID`, then a `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` binding, then this file, then the directory name; a binding that disagrees with the file is refused as `conflicting-project-binding`, a malformed file as `invalid-project-id`, and `pose doctor` reports both under `project.identity`. Declared once, the id is the same in every checkout, under any directory name, for the CLI and the MCP server.\n- [`.pose/templates/roadmap.md`](.pose/templates/roadmap.md): governed roadmap template.\n- [`.pose/templates/changelog-fragment.md`](.pose/templates/changelog-fragment.md): user-facing fragment per spec (written at closeout).\n- [`.pose/workflows/release.md`](.pose/workflows/release.md): immutable release preparation, publication reconciliation and verification.\n\nRelease cuts use `pose release plan`, `prepare`, `check`, `notes`, `record`,\n`status`, `open-next` and `backfill`. A tag is not publication; terminal release\nconfidence comes only from provider and independent-verification evidence.\n- [`.pose/templates/doc-audit-report.md`](.pose/templates/doc-audit-report.md): template for editorial reviews and documentation audits.\n- `pose` binary: native scaffold/check/validate/report automation and MCP server.\n- [`.pose/specs/*/spec.md`](.pose/specs/): living specs per feature.\n- Native project-state artifact, generated by `pose state init` (`pose state`/`pose_project_state`) — optional, absent on a freshly-installed project; configurable staleness policy (optional, defaults 7 days / 20 commits — see §6).\n- [`.agents/skills/`](.agents/skills/): 11 skills in Codex-native format (`name`/`description` frontmatter, body with Required reading + Steps + Output requirements, optional metadata in `agents/openai.yaml`). Use `description` as the single routing source; Claude Code consumes the symlinks in [`.claude/skills/`](.claude/skills/) without requiring `when_to_use`.\n\n---\n\n## 5) Flows per task type\n\nThe operational step-by-step lives in the workflows. Each workflow also\nincludes the relevant \"Execution — planner/implementer/reviewer mode\" sections.\n\n- Feature: [`.pose/workflows/feature.md`](.pose/workflows/feature.md)\n- Bugfix: [`.pose/workflows/bugfix.md`](.pose/workflows/bugfix.md)\n- Review: [`.pose/workflows/review.md`](.pose/workflows/review.md)\n- Refactor: [`.pose/workflows/refactor.md`](.pose/workflows/refactor.md)\n- Documentation: [`.pose/workflows/documentation-update.md`](.pose/workflows/documentation-update.md)\n- Recurrence escalation: [`.pose/workflows/recurrence-escalation.md`](.pose/workflows/recurrence-escalation.md)\n\nThe agent contract (precedence, spec/ADR/check obligations, verification,\ndon'ts) lives in [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md) and is **not** repeated here.\n\n### 5.1 Spec lifecycle\n\nEvery spec created by `pose new-spec`\ncarries frontmatter with state and dates, preventing specs that linger \"open\"\nafter completion and follow-ups that rot into dead text.\n\n```yaml\n---\nslug: <feature-slug>\nstatus: draft        # draft → in-progress → done | blocked | superseded | abandoned\ncreated_at: 2026-01-15   # stamped by pose new-spec\ncompleted_at:            # filled on the transition to done\nsupersedes:              # slug of the superseded spec (when applicable)\ndepends_on:              # prerequisites: other-spec, milestone:<roadmap>/<id>, roadmap:<slug>\npriority:                # integer >= 0 (lower = higher priority)\n---\n```\n\n- **`status`** evolves `draft` → `in-progress` → `done`. Alternative terminal\n  states: `superseded` (use `supersedes:` on the successor) and `abandoned`.\n  `blocked` is not terminal: it is an operational condition a spec can leave.\n  Readiness keeps a blocked spec not ready, still resolves its `depends_on`\n  and reports `cause: dependency` or `cause: unknown`; spec transfer also uses\n  `blocked` while a destination is staged. It is never a delivery outcome, and\n  no command rewrites a legacy `blocked` spec to `in-progress`.\n- **`created_at`/`completed_at`** give the spec's real time window (file mtime\n  is unreliable — it changes on every edit).\n- **`depends_on`** declares prerequisites as an **inline comma-separated\n  list** (POSE frontmatter is flat by contract — never a multi-line YAML\n  list), with typed refs: a spec slug, `milestone:<roadmap>/<id>` or\n  `roadmap:<slug>`. Spec refs are resolved by `check` (existence + graph\n  acyclicity); `milestone:`/`roadmap:` refs resolve against the governed\n  roadmaps in `.pose/roadmaps/` when they exist. `depends_on` expresses a real\n  technical/logical prerequisite; scheduling preference is `priority`'s job.\n  The aggregated graph lives in\n  [`.pose/indexes/spec-graph.json`](.pose/indexes/) (generated by\n  `pose index`; frontmatter stays authoritative) and a spec's eligibility is\n  queryable via the pose-mcp tool `pose_spec_readiness`.\n- **`priority`** (optional) orders attack preference among eligible specs; it\n  creates no blocking.\n- **Follow-ups with disposition:** the `Final Report > Follow-ups` section is\n  not free text. Each item gets a bracketed disposition — `[open]`,\n  `[spawned: <slug>]`, `[covered: <slug>]`, `[duplicate: <slug>]`, `[done]`,\n  `[wont-do: <reason>]`. That answers, per follow-up, whether it seeded a new\n  spec, is already covered elsewhere, was triaged before, or was discarded.\n  Open items additionally declare ownership and a triage service level with a\n  trailing `(owner:@alias crit:low|medium|high review:YYYY-MM-DD)` group —\n  the SLA is a triage promise, not an implementation deadline. That group is\n  the only format read: it must be the last thing on the bullet, in\n  parentheses, and the bullet may wrap. Ownership written any other way is\n  ignored, so the item reads as `unowned` and never becomes overdue;\n  `pose lint-spec` warns about it. Legacy items without the group are reported\n  as `unowned` (warning at closeout).\n- **Requirement trace:** at closeout, the `Validation > Requirement trace`\n  subsection maps every declared `R<N>` to its outcome — `[satisfied]` with\n  evidence (free text plus structured refs `check:`, `test:`, `report:`,\n  `commit:`), `[waived: <reason>]` or `[withdrawn: <reason>]`. Orphaned or\n  missing IDs fail `lint-spec --strict`; the MCP tool\n  `pose_requirement_trace` exposes the bidirectional projection. A `test:` ref\n  must name a test that exists in the repository, its submodules or a nested\n  module repository the validation matrix registers: a Go test function or\n  subtest, a JavaScript/TypeScript test title (exact or in slug form), a Python\n  `test_` or Rust `#[test]` function, a tracked test file, or a `go test -run`\n  pattern that selects a test. An unresolved ref fails `lint-spec` and\n  `pose close` while the spec is open, and is only reported on a closed spec.\n\nCloseout is an explicit step (skill [`pose-spec-closeout`](.agents/skills/pose-spec-closeout/SKILL.md)):\nset `status: done`, fill `completed_at`, triage every follow-up and pass the\ngate `pose lint-spec <slug> --strict`,\nwhich blocks \"done without `completed_at`\" and \"done with an undispositioned\nfollow-up\". The aggregated live backlog (`pose followups --open`) feeds the\nplanning of new specs.\n\nFollow-up triage has **two layers**, by design, to keep the CLI deterministic\nand avoid cascading drift:\n\n1. **Deterministic (CLI):** `pose followups`\n   proposes near-duplicate candidates by lexical similarity. Reproducible, no\n   network, runs in CI.\n2. **Semantic + confirmation (agent):** the `pose-spec-closeout` skill judges\n   intent equivalence (what lexical heuristics miss) and **confirms with the\n   user before writing** the consequential dispositions\n   (`[spawned]`/`[covered]`/`[duplicate]`) — reusing a follow-up is a\n   decision, not a default.\n\n---\n\n## 6) The `pose` CLI\n\n```bash\npose help                          # show help\n\n# Scaffold and specs\npose init [--wizard [--yes]]       # install here when no instance exists (installer\n                                   # flags pass through), else confirm it is installed;\n                                   # --wizard then detects stacks and seeds the matrix\npose specs [--recent [N]] [--status S] [--since YYYY-MM-DD|<days>d] [--components tags] [--json]\n                                   # list and discover specs chronologically\npose spec-format <migrate <slug>|--all [--format folder|flat] [--dry-run]|status> [--json]\n                                   # inspect and migrate specs to chronological layout\npose spec-transfer preview --source <xref> --destination <xref> --map R1=equivalent:R1 [--json]\npose spec-transfer apply --plan <file> --digest <sha256> --authorize-project <id>...\npose spec-transfer resume --operation <id> --project <id> --authorize-project <id>...\npose spec-transfer status --operation <id> [--project <id>]\n                                   # explicit, digest-bound authority transfer across projects\npose context [--project-id <id>] [--task <artifact-ref>] [--json]\n                                   # path-free selected project, authority and revision\npose new-spec <slug> [--folder|--legacy] [--task <xref>] [--expect-context <digest>]\n                                   # reuse canonical authority or create in its explicit project\npose new-spec-qualified <slug> --task <xref> --expect-context <digest>\n                                   # qualified creation; old engines reject the distinct verb\npose new-roadmap <slug>            # create a governed roadmap in .pose/roadmaps/\npose new-adr \"<title>\"             # create a dated ADR\npose new-knowledge <type> <slug>   # create handoff/note/decision-log\n                                   # (options: --owner @x --ttl-days N --restricted)\n# `pose lint-spec <slug> --design-check` projects structured Assumption/Decision\n# basis nodes read-only; it is advisory and never changes lifecycle state.\n\n# Deterministic gates\npose check [--strict|--tolerant]   # structural integrity + matrix schema +\n                                   # task-map sync + spec graph + schema version\npose validate [--strict|--tolerant] [--stack s] [--module path] [--report] [--json-out f] [--junit f] [--sarif f]\n              [--changed-from rev [--changed-to rev]] [--jobs n] [--explain] [--emit-plan f]\npose lint-spec <slug>|--all [--strict|--tolerant] [--required-only] [--ready-check] [--design-check]\npose knowledge-check [--strict|--tolerant] [--max-overdue N]\npose recurrence-check [--strict|--tolerant] [--window-days N] [--threshold T] [--flap-threshold N] [--include-pass]\npose history-check [--strict|--tolerant]\npose skills-check [--strict|--tolerant]\n# every gate above, plus `pose index` and `pose state`, also takes: [--json] [--json-out f] [--quiet] [--color auto|always|never]\npose public-claims [--strict|--tolerant] [--json]\n                                   # public surfaces vs. the released fact:\n                                   # stale version, evergreen surface that\n                                   # gained one, non-canonical docs host\npose artifact-check --spec <slug> [--from <rev> --to <rev>] [--strict|--tolerant] [--json]\npose surface-check [--spec <slug>] [--results <path>] [--strict|--tolerant] [--json]\npose roadmap-check <slug> [--strict|--tolerant] [--json]\npose docs-check [--json] [--explain <rule>]\n\n# Governed closeout and review\npose followups [--open|--all] [--json] [--owner <alias>] [--overdue] [--similarity N] [--fail-overdue]\npose review-plan <scope|xref> [--json] [--explain]\npose review bundle <scope|xref> [--json] [--explain] [--seal [--lead <vendor>[/<model>]]] [--expect-context <digest>]\npose review attest <bundle-id> --reviewer <execution-id> --decision <decision> --evidence <ref>\n                   [--criterion <c>] [--tool <t>] [--finding <f>] [--plan-digest <sha>] [--apply]\npose review attest --envelope <project-relative-path> [--apply]\npose review auto-attest <bundle-id|scope-ref> [--reviewer <id>] [--apply]\npose review verify <scope|xref|bundle-id|bundle-path> [--json]\npose review-check <scope|xref> [--json]\npose closeout-check <scope|xref> [--json]\npose review record <scope|xref> --reviewer <execution-id> --decision <decision> --evidence <ref> [--apply] [--expect-context <digest>]\npose close <scope|xref> [--expect-context <digest>]\npose continuous-closeout <start <scope> [--apply]|status [--json]|complete --apply>\npose artifact-backfill --from-git [--apply --confirm-spec-edits]\n\n# Delegated executions\npose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke <scope|bundle-id> [--agent auto|<name>] [--label <name>] [--lead <vendor>] [--reason <text>] [--note-file <path>] [--dry-run] [--json]\npose delegate task <prompt-file|-> --agent <name> [--cd <dir>] [--access read|sandboxed|write|full] [--effort <level>] [--detach] [--dry-run] [--json]\npose delegate ls [<run>|<bundle-id>] [--active] [--json]\npose delegate watch [<run>] [-v] [--no-follow]\npose delegate wait <run> [--timeout <d>] [--json]\npose delegate resume <run> [--wait-until-reset[=<max>]] [--dry-run] [--json]\npose delegate handoff <run> --to <agent|auto> [--dry-run] [--json]\npose delegate cancel <run> [--json]\npose delegate agents [--json]\npose runtime serve|start|attach|status|stop|restart [--idle <d>] [--drain <d>] [--json]  # optional per-worktree runtime; opt-in, standalone without it\npose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <review-check|closeout-check|artifact-check|surface-check|roadmap-check> [args...]  # runs the check in the runtime when there is one, else standalone\n\n# Project state and docs governance\npose state [init|refresh [--if-stale]|diff]  # native project-state artifact; no subcommand = validate\npose state --governance [--scope <ref>] [--json]  # live: supported, configured, applicable and effective governance\npose state --attention [--scope <ref>] [--actor <id|role>] [--phase <p>] [--kind <c>] [--json]  # live: what is owed, by whom, restricting which phase\npose docs-init [--profile library|service|cli|monorepo]\npose docs-review <resolve <doc> [--no-change --reason <text>] [--commit <sha>]|request <doc>|--all-stale>\npose docs-sync [--dry-run]\n\n# Discovery and metrics\npose suggest [<type>] [--domain <d>] [--path <p>] [--json]\npose stats [workflows|tasks|contexts] [--since-days N] [--json]\npose insights [...]                # alias of stats; includes delegated draft metrics\npose stats governance [--since-days N] [--maturity-days N] [--min-sample N] [--band baseline|elevated|critical|unknown] [--report-type standard|doc-audit|unknown] [--waits] [--rework] [--outcomes] [--json]\npose usage [--since-days N] [--tool NAME] [--surface cli|mcp] [--json]\npose stacks [--path dir] [--json]  # read-only catalog of detected profiles\npose recurrence-effect [--register ...] [--json] [--fail-ineffective]\npose record-deployment --environment <env> --deployment-kind <kind> [...]\npose record-incident --environment <env> [...]\npose dora-metrics [--application <app>] [--environment <env>] [--window-days N] [--json]\npose adoption-metrics [--json]\npose semantic-suggest <query> | pose knowledge-suggest <query>\npose suggest-feedback | pose portfolio-projection | pose reconcile-evidence\n\n# Assessment and extensions\npose assess <discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot> [--json] [--update-state]\npose extension <install|list|remove|verify> [...]\n\n# Artifact generation and maintenance\npose index [--verify]              # regenerate indexes as one generation (--verify: read-only check)\npose report --task \"...\" [--outcome pass|fail|partial|skipped|unknown] [--since <ref>] [--git-stage] [...]\npose amend <slug> [...]            # record an append-only spec amendment\npose start spec:<slug> [--json|--apply --digest <sha256> [--json]|--status [--json]|--cancel|--recover-lock <owner digest> [--json]]  # atomic start of a draft spec\npose adopt --list | <capability> [--off|--decline|--defer --reason <text>] [--date YYYY-MM-DD] [--apply] | --request <act-id> [--apply]  # decide a capability from the catalog\npose setup [--json] [--no-input]  # what is in force, new and missing; the next step, confirmed one at a time\npose identity add [<principal>] --key <file.pub> [--role <role>] [--apply] | list [--json] | remove <principal> [--fingerprint <fp>] [--apply]  # keys that prove answers\npose action <open|list|show|statement|resolve|cancel|waive|invalidate> ...  # open, read and resolve material requests to a person or an external system\npose issuer init <name> | pose issuer pin <name|name:public-key> [--attestations] [--human-authority] [--audience <id>] [--apply] | pose issuer list [--json]\n                                   # native issuers that sign review attestations; keys live outside the project\npose migrate v7 --dry-run [--json]  # dry-run the legacy inventory a future major would convert, keep or refuse\npose knowledge-housekeeping <list-expired|archive-expired|purge-archived> [--dry-run|--apply]\npose knowledge-usage [--json]\npose reports-housekeeping <list-stale|archive-stale|purge-archived> [--older-than N] [--dry-run|--apply]\npose events-housekeeping <list-expired|archive-expired|purge-archived> [--dry-run|--apply]\npose hooks <install|uninstall|status> [--force]\n\n# Installation, MCP, contributions and telemetry\npose version                       # binary and instance schema versions\npose install <dir> [--locale tag]  # install the embedded POSE without cloning\npose update [--dry-run] [--force] [--no-self] [--locale tag]\n                                   # updates the binary, merges POSE.md/AGENTS.md,\n                                   # delivers machinery, migrates the schema;\n                                   # --force also resets the manuals wholesale\npose import <spec-kit|openspec> <path> [--dry-run]\npose serve-mcp --stdio             # local transport managed by the MCP client\npose serve-mcp                     # HTTP server; set the POSE_* variables first\npose doctor [--json] [--fix]       # diagnoses the binary, local configuration and\n                                   # governance that would fail only downstream;\n                                   # proves no live connection — use pose_mcp_context;\n                                   # levels: ok, next (a recommended step, such as the\n                                   # pre-commit hook or naming action roles — counted\n                                   # apart, never a failure), warn, error\npose report-limitation --title \"...\" --kind limitation|bug|suggestion [--body \"...\"] [--submit]\n                                   # without --submit, writes only to .pose/feedback/\npose contribute <enable|disable|status|stage|list> [--target <dir>] [--json]\n                                   # open-source contributor mode; stages sanitized\n                                   # reports under .pose/contributions/ without leaking\n                                   # proprietary code; submission remains developer-adjudicated\npose telemetry <enable|disable|status>\n\n# Release lifecycle\npose release <plan|prepare|check|notes|record|status|open-next|backfill> --version vX.Y.Z\n                                   # prepare freezes manifest/notes; record imports\n                                   # provider evidence; status projects the state\npose release-notes --version vX.Y.Z  # compatibility alias for the immutable notes\npose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out <dir>  # package-manager manifests of a release\n```\n\n### Command reference\n\n- `init` — ensures the minimal required `.pose` directory structure, policy files and indexes. Supports `--wizard` to auto-detect repository stacks and seed the initial validation matrix.\n- `check` — validates POSE structural integrity (required paths and references in `AGENTS.md`/`POSE.md`), and that the published index set is whole (an error in strict mode, a warning in tolerant mode, naming `pose index`, when the generation descriptor does not describe its files; a stale index relative to the sources is not reported) **plus** the [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json) schema, [`task-map.json`](.pose/indexes/task-map.json) sync, the native spec dependency graph and the schema-version gate. It fails in `--strict` and warns where permitted in `--tolerant`.\n- `specs` — lists and discovers repository specifications sorted chronologically (newest first). Supports `--recent <N>`, `--status <status>`, `--since <duration|date>`, `--components <tags>`, and `--json`. An unknown flag, a missing value, an unparseable, negative or out-of-range `--since` and a non-positive `--recent` exit 2 before anything is listed (spec `pose-cli-mcp-operation-results`); a bare `--recent` means 10. `specs facts <slug>` takes `--json` in any position and refuses anything else. MCP `pose_list_specs` refuses the same values, an unknown argument, an explicit `0` for `recent` or `limit`, an explicit `null` and a `sort` other than `date` or `slug`. With `--json`, an operational failure of `specs`, `specs facts`, `start` or `close --plan` is one operation result with outcome `failed` and a typed cause, exit 1.\n- `spec-format` — inspects and migrates specifications to the modern chronological date-prefixed format (`migrate <slug>|--all [--format folder|flat] [--dry-run]`, `status`). Enforces mandatory directory envelope preservation whenever companion artifacts (`amendments.jsonl`) exist.\n- `spec-transfer` — explicit cross-project authority transfer. `preview` emits a deterministic digest-bound JSON plan without writing. Requirement mappings are explicit (`R1=equivalent:R1`, `R2=withdrawn`, `-=pending:R3`); apply requires the same digest plus one `--authorize-project` for every affected repository, and resume verifies the saved plan copies and receipts. Transfer is refused until both endpoints and every affected project adopt review-policy schema 4 (`qualified_artifact_refs_version: 1`, `spec_authority_transfer_version: 1`). Current dependency/membership links follow the verified redirect; the MCP tool `pose_spec_transfer_status` reads the journal only within its authorized project. `--mode reconcile-terminal` retires a coordinator spec onto an executor that is already `done` with a terminal closeout in its own project: the executor file is never written (apply only verifies its digest), the map is N:M over every source requirement and may target another spec with `destination_ref`, `withdrawn` needs a `rationale`, and `pending` needs a `rationale` plus an open target spec, recorded in the schema-2 redirect as `open_obligations`; `--map-file` reads the map as JSON. Engines without this mode refuse schema-2 plans and redirects. No automatic commits, pushes or policy adoption occur. Split-section specs without a single `spec.md` are reported as unsupported rather than rewritten.\n- `new-spec` — creates a new spec with standard lifecycle frontmatter and 7 engineering sections. By default writes the dated flat file `.pose/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md`; `--folder` writes `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>/spec.md` and `--legacy` writes `<slug>/spec.md`, all under [`.pose/specs/`](.pose/specs/).\n- `new-adr` — creates an ADR with the standard template and a deterministic slug.\n- `new-roadmap` — creates a governed roadmap in `.pose/roadmaps/` from [`.pose/templates/roadmap.md`](.pose/templates/roadmap.md): flat frontmatter (`status: draft|active|done|abandoned`, `depends_on:` between roadmaps) + milestones as `## Milestone: <id>` sections with flat bullets (`- after:`, `- target_start:`, `- target_due:`, `- specs:`). `check` validates single membership in active roadmaps, milestone/roadmap DAGs, dates and typed-ref resolution; `pose_spec_readiness` resolves those refs for real (milestone satisfied = its specs done; roadmap satisfied = status done). Dates are planning input; actuals derive from events.\n- `new-knowledge` — creates an artifact in [`.pose/knowledge/`](.pose/knowledge/) from [`.pose/templates/knowledge.md`](.pose/templates/knowledge.md) with mandatory frontmatter (`type`, `owner`, `sensitivity`, `created_at`, `last_reviewed_at`, `expires_at`). Computes `expires_at` from the TTL (default 30d, max 90d).\n- `validate` — runs the declarative matrix in [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json): per-stack checks, per-module overrides, severity (`required`/`optional`) and mode (`strict`/`tolerant`). `--json-out`/`--junit`/`--sarif <path>` (`--json <path>` still works as a deprecated alias of `--json-out <path>`) emit the versioned structured result (schema 1) from one canonical model: stable check IDs (`<module>/<stack>/<name>`), command metadata, timing, severity, distinguishable outcomes (`pass|fail|error|skipped` — infra failures never masquerade as check failures), deterministic skip reasons, bounded captured output and secret redaction (configured env values only; inherited environment never enters the result). Text output stays authoritative; machine formats are additive. POSE-specific semantics survive the JUnit/SARIF projections via documented extensions (classname suffix / `pose/*` properties).\n  **Runtime guardrails:** every check runs under a timeout (`timeoutSeconds` per check, `defaults.timeoutSeconds`, safe default 600s) and an output ceiling (`defaults.maxOutputBytes`, default 1 MiB); breaching either terminates the process group and records the explicit state (`limit_state: timeout|output-limit`). Checks marked `isolation: \"required\"` never run locally — they are skipped with a machine-readable reason and exported by `--emit-plan <file>`: an execution-plan envelope binding project, spec, check plan, matrix digest, git HEAD and an approval slot to be stamped with an expiring execution identity before the Harness may run it.\n  **Changed scope:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` selects the minimum safe module set deterministically — modules containing changed files (tracked and untracked), transitive dependents via `dependsOn` edges in [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json), and policy widening (criticality `high` always runs). A change outside every module runs everything (uncertainty prefers safe execution); unselected checks are recorded as skipped with the selection reason and `--explain` prints every decision. Revisions are confined to a safe grammar; without the flags, full validation is unchanged.\n\n  **Concurrency:** checks run up to `--jobs <n>` at a time (default: half the cores, bounded by available memory at about 1.5 GiB per check and by 8); results are reported and recorded in matrix order, whatever order they finish in, and the report records `jobs` and `wall_seconds`. `--jobs 1` and `--verbose` run one at a time. A check marked `exclusive: true` runs with nothing else running: earlier checks finish first, so its `when` can depend on them, which a concurrent check's cannot — a check's `when` is read as it starts. The run ends with its wall time beside the sum of its checks and the slowest checks. An interrupt or SIGTERM stops every running check's process group (on Windows, its process) and starts nothing more; those checks are recorded with `limit_state: cancelled`, the run is `fail` and exits 1, and a second signal acts at once. A default `--jobs` follows the cgroup's memory headroom and CPU quota where there is one.\n\n  **Empty Go checks:** a `go test` check that selects tests with `-run` or `-skip` and exits 0 fails with `limit_state: no-tests` when Go's result lines report every package as having run nothing, since Go exits 0 when a pattern selects nothing. A check over several packages passes when any of them reports running tests. The rule reads Go's summary and is no proof that a test ran; only a plain form is judged — packages, then `-run`, `-skip`, `-count`, `-timeout`, `-parallel`, `-cpu`, `-outputdir`, `-v`, `-short`, `-failfast`, `-race`, `-cover`, `-covermode`, `-coverpkg`, `-tags` and `-p` — and any other flag, `-args`, `--`, a positional argument after the packages or quoted `GOFLAGS` leaves a check with Go's own outcome.\n  **Evidence classes:** every check declares the `evidenceClass` it produces from one closed vocabulary — `build`, `unit`, `integration`, `e2e`, `reachability`, `a11y`, `design-system`, `contrast`, `visual-regression`, `lint`, `typecheck`, `security-scan`, `contract`, `structure`, `smoke` — the same vocabulary review profiles may demand; a check outside it is refused, and a check with no class contributes nothing when a review collects evidence. `--report` writes a report listing the commands this run executed and its result, and derives the outcome from them.\n- `update` — single-command complete update: automatically checks and updates the `pose` binary executable to the latest GitHub release, refreshes scaffolds, rules, workflows and MCP config (`--force`), and migrates the repository instance schema (`.pose/schema-version`). A plain update merges `POSE.md`/`AGENTS.md` (instance-owned sections keep their content; an instance edit a merge cannot keep is saved to `<file>.pose-backup`, and a section that still matches what POSE last wrote is simply replaced), delivers machinery — backing up only files the instance edited since POSE delivered them, as recorded by digest in `.pose/state/machinery-manifest.json` — and seeds absent instance config; `--force` additionally reruns `install` over the instance, which resets the manuals wholesale. Use `--dry-run` to preview or `--no-self` to skip the binary self-update. A run that delivered its files but finds the instance's own state invalid reports that through the final gate and does not roll back. Downgrade is always refused.\n- `index` — generates `repo-map.json`, `services.json`, `packages.json`, `spec-graph.json` and `roadmaps.json` (the specs' `depends_on`/`priority` graph, cached for pose-mcp) in `.pose/indexes/`, including per-module operational metadata from [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json).\n- `report` — generates a versionable report in `.pose/reports/` with execution metadata, minimal per-task history (`.pose/reports/history/`) and stable-field diffs.\n- `knowledge-check` — double gate: (1) validates each artifact's frontmatter in [`.pose/knowledge/`](.pose/knowledge/) against the rule (`type`, `sensitivity`, `expires_at`, TTL ≤ 90d), and (2) counts the overdue backlog against `--max-overdue`. In `--strict` both gates exit 1.\n- `recurrence-check` — scans [`history JSONL`](.pose/reports/) for `task_slug`s with `≥ --threshold` occurrences in `--window-days` (default 3 in 14d). Ignores `outcome=pass` by default (problematic recurrence is repeated failure), but a `pass` settles the earlier failures of its own `stable_hash` cluster: only failures after a cluster's latest `pass`, or with no `pass` in the window, count, and a pass of another `stable_hash` settles nothing. Settled clusters are not flagged and are listed as `resolved` findings with `recurrence.resolved_clusters`; `--include-pass` counts every record as before. A task whose consecutive runs switch between passing and not passing at least `--flap-threshold` times in the window (default 4) is reported as a `flapping` warning with `recurrence.flapping_keys` (spec `pose-recurrence-flapping-signal`); it never changes the exit code, because an unstable outcome is a signal to investigate, not a recurrence. When flagged, points to [`recurrence-escalation.md`](.pose/workflows/recurrence-escalation.md).\n- `recurrence-effect` — closes the feedback edge: `--register` ties an escalation to its intervention (`rule:|workflow:|spec:<name>`) and observation window in append-only `interventions.jsonl`; the report compares recurrence rate (and optional `pose report --duration-seconds/--cost-usd` telemetry) before/after per intervention with data-quality warnings (sparse sample, incomplete window). `INEFFECTIVE` verdicts demand a governed follow-up; `--fail-ineffective` makes that blocking by policy. Aggregation is by task/context only — never individuals.\n- `extension install|list|remove|verify` — signed extension lifecycle (spec pose-extension-catalog-lifecycle): a package is a directory with `extension.json` (id, version, kind: `skill|workflow|rule|import-adapter`, `pose_schema_range`, `files`, `permissions`, optional `conflicts_with`, `provenance`) plus `files/<repo-relative-target>` for every declared path. Targets are confined to `.agents/skills/`, `.pose/workflows/`, `.pose/rules/`, `.pose/templates/` and must fall inside the package's own declared `permissions`. Extensions are **data only** — the lifecycle never executes anything from a package. `install`/`remove` are dry-runnable (`--dry-run`), require explicit consent (`--yes`) and are transactional: any mid-transaction failure rolls back every file already written. A conflicting target (owned by another extension, or an existing untracked file) blocks the operation unless `--force`; a locally modified managed file blocks `remove` unless `--force` (user modifications are preserved by default). Unsigned packages are rejected unless `--allow-unsigned` is passed explicitly; signature verification runs `cosign verify-blob` against the identity the package's own `provenance.signer`/`provenance.issuer` declare. Installed extensions are recorded in `.pose/indexes/extensions.lock.json` (id, version, per-file digest, provenance, signature status) — read-only over MCP via `pose_extension_list`; `install`/`remove` stay CLI-only by design (POSE never exposes general-purpose write tools over MCP). A `revoked: true` manifest is always rejected.\n- `skills-check` — Agent Skills conformance gate (spec pose-agent-skills-conformance): every `.agents/skills/<slug>/SKILL.md` must declare `name` (matching its directory), `description`, `when_to_use` plus POSE's additive compatibility metadata `pose_schema_range` (`\"min-max\"` against `.pose/schema-version`), `clients` and `capabilities` (comma-separated). Every relative markdown link must resolve inside the repository (no path escape); content is scanned offline for unsafe instructions (`curl | sh`-style, `--no-verify`, disabled TLS verification) and secret-shaped strings (defense in depth, not a replacement for the dedicated secret-scanning gate). Declaring the `claude-code` client requires a real `.claude/skills` symlink in `scaffold.ClaudeSkillLinks`. `--strict`/`--tolerant` mirror `check`.\n- `lint-spec` — verifies each `spec.md` section has real content, not placeholders. **`--ready-check`** applies the **Definition of Ready** (ENTRY gate): Intent/Requirements/Technical Plan filled, acceptance criteria with stable IDs (`- R<N>:`) and syntactically valid `depends_on` — without requiring Validation/Final Report. The Definition of Ready is opt-in: it applies to specs created on or after `adopted_at` in `.pose/policy/dor.json` (shipped empty), and `task_type` in the spec's frontmatter selects which sections it requires; once adopted, `check` applies it on the `→ in-progress` transition. Use `--all` to audit every spec; `--required-only` skips the optional `Decisions` section. **Lifecycle gate:** `status: done` requires `completed_at` and a valid disposition on every follow-up; for `spawned`/`covered`/`duplicate` the target must be an **existing** spec (and not itself). A target in another project is written qualified, `[covered: xref:<project>/spec:<slug>]`: when that project is bound (`POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS`) the spec must exist there, and when it is not bound the target is accepted, because the other repository being absent is not evidence the spec is missing. In any status it warns when an open follow-up's ownership is written outside the trailing `(owner:@alias crit:low|medium|high review:YYYY-MM-DD)` group, and it reads a wrapped follow-up bullet as one item, like `followups`.\n- `followups` — aggregates all specs' follow-ups, derives the live (`--open`) or full (`--all`) backlog, projects ownership (`--owner <alias>`) and expired reviews (`--overdue`), and proposes near-duplicate candidates by deterministic lexical similarity (token Jaccard + `SequenceMatcher`, stdlib only; threshold via `--similarity 0..100`, default 60). Exit 0 by default, no network; `--fail-overdue` turns expired reviews into a blocking, risk-based policy gate. The candidates are mechanical hints — the **semantic judgement** and the **confirmation of reuse** live in the agent layer (skill `pose-spec-closeout`), never in this script. Source coverage (spec `pose-source-coverage-integrity`): the read reports what it could not see. `--json` carries `coverage_complete` and `coverage` (each loss names the artifact, the kind, and the spec it belongs to when the file name or a readable header shows it), the human output prints `coverage=INCOMPLETE` and each unread file, and `--fail-overdue` fails instead of certifying \"none overdue\" over an incomplete read. The backlog, `--candidates`, the project state and `pose state --attention` count one unresolved set: an explicit `[open]` and a bullet with no disposition (Attention projects the latter as `followup-undisposed`). The same applies to `pose assess tech-debt`, whose report states the files scanned, the ones it could not read (incomplete) and the ones it leaves out on purpose (over the size limit, binary, generated), and finds a marker after a very long line; and to the review history: `pose review-check` and `pose review verify` do not take the latest readable attempt or attestation as the current one while an unreadable record that may be later exists, and `pose review record` refuses to choose the attempt it supersedes from a history with a hole. The obligation report marks a producer `incomplete` when it lost an element (an obligation refused by validation, a spec or review record it could not read).\n- `review-plan` / `review-check` / `closeout-check` — resolve a deterministic component-aware plan from governed metadata, typed overlays and a closed native-tool catalog, then validate immutable attempts against both scope and plan digests. Opt in explicitly with review-policy schema v2, `component_aware: true` and `component_aware_adopted_at`; preview the migration read-only with `pose review-plan <scope> --explain` before committing the policy. Completed attempts older than that component adoption date remain auditable, while open scopes require the new plan. `pose review record` is dry-run by default, accepts `--plan-digest` to reject drift and appends with `--apply`; recommended tools are never executed implicitly. Hierarchical closeout rolls approvals through `spec:`, `milestone:` and `roadmap:` scopes. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_plan`, `pose_closeout_state`. The JSON of `closeout-check` and `pose_closeout_state` types its blockers in `diagnostics` (code, domain, refs, satisfaction condition; text blockers the engine cannot type yet are carried as `opaque`) and its next action in `next_step`; readiness `waiting_on` entries carry a `code`, and auto-attest pendencies a `code`, `source` and `condition` (spec `pose-typed-producer-diagnostics`). The prose fields stay for people; consumers read the codes.\n- `review bundle` / `review attest` / `review auto-attest` / `review verify` / `review record` — opt-in fixed-point review (`review_bundles: true` plus an adoption date). Evaluation is a DAG, and each stage names the one before it: the implementation subject carries an `implementation_digest` over its classified content alone — not over change-set ids or refs, so a squash or rebase of the same content keeps its identity — and an observer of the implementation anchors there rather than on the bundle that will contain it. Sealed evidence records `subject_observation` as `observed`, `carried-forward` or `unknown`; a result naming no commit is unknown, never current. A change set's range observation reports how many commits `base..head` spans against how many it attributes, because paths resolved from trailers are attributed while the range between them is only provenance; it sits outside the sealed payload, so an unrelated commit never stales a review of content that did not move. A criterion is `mechanical` when it names evidence classes a registered check emits, and `judgment` otherwise: `auto-attest` answers the first kind from the sealed evidence and reports the second as pending, refusing `--apply` until a reviewer records a conclusion for each. A profile may declare `kind` explicitly to ask for judgment on a criterion a check also covers; it may not declare `mechanical` on one naming no evidence class. A component-scoped tool whose component the validation matrix declares runs no check (`replaceDefaultChecks` with an empty list) is planned with `producer_coverage: none` and may be disposed `not-used` with a reason, because nothing in the repository can emit what it asks for; everywhere else a required tool must still pass. Preparation hashes the semantic Intent, Requirements, Technical Plan and Decisions, attributed patch/tree content, consumed plan inputs, validation identities and child bundles. Tasks, execution logs, Final Report, lifecycle metadata, attestations and derived state remain outside the review digest but retain their own gates. `--seal` persists immutable JSON in the review-bundles directory under an `rvb-` identity; attestations are separate append-only JSON in review-attestations under an `rva-` identity. `auto-attest` extracts matching evidence from validation results and resolves tool dispositions deterministically. Semantic or source changes create a superseding bundle and typed delta; derived closeout changes do not cause rereview. Local review stays offline. Conductor may return an optional Ed25519 envelope only when its issuer/public-key pin is trusted by policy. Human plan output groups repeated path warnings and separates active required, recommended and completion-deferred tools; JSON retains full provenance. Sealing also records the governance contracts in force (`component-aware`, `review-bundles`, `evidence-vocabulary`) and the gates that decide the bundle (reservations, accepted-risk severities, criterion reuse); verification reads them from the bundle, and only `require_signed_attestations` is read live. Sealing warns about evidence produced against a commit other than the approved head. Sealing also records the implementation identity: principal, vendor, model, a digest of the sealing session's reference (never the reference) and whether it was `detected` from the session's agent markers or `declared` with `--seal --lead <vendor>[/<model>]`. The model comes from `--lead` or `routing.lead_models` and is `unknown` otherwise, which never establishes a model difference; markers of two vendors leave the vendor `unknown`. Resealing content a sealed bundle already holds returns that bundle with the identity it recorded, so a reviewer closing out never becomes the implementation; only a `--lead` naming another identity than the governing bundle's seals anew, and that seal names the bundle it replaces, so it governs whatever the seal instants say; a run compared with a replaced identity stops governing. The identity is sealed but is not content: a sealed bundle matches a preparation that differs from it only by the identity. An attestation's `passed` criterion must cite evidence the bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts (any one of its `evidence_classes`), from the component it is about — evidence from a directory inside a component does not answer for the component. `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` records `passed`, `not-applicable` (rationale required) or `finding` (names a recorded finding); `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]` needs a severity and an action, and `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` needs a sealed accepted severity, an owner, a rationale and a review date. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_bundle`. `pose_review_prepare` (read-only) returns the auto-attest draft and its pending judgment criteria; `pose_review_attest` (spec `pose-mcp-review-attest-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an attestation over MCP only inside a trusted issuer's envelope, the proof `--envelope` verifies; its preview completes a draft for a sealed bundle (bundle digest, attested time, id and, when it names `confirmed_by`, the confirmation digest) and returns the exact bytes to sign. A human confirmation is disclosed as verified only when the signed authority claim names that human.\n  **Raw history:** every history reader of structural review and of the delivery graph ignores replace refs and grafts; a repository with either gets other review inputs than before, so rerun `pose index` and reseal. A trailer change set that starts at the repository's first commit resolves against the empty tree.\n  **Removal witnesses:** a review subject path a later spec removed reads as that removal only while Git still shows it: the deleting commit on `HEAD`, read raw, with no later commit touching the path, in a clone that is not shallow. Otherwise preparation refuses and asks for `pose index`. A generation binds the change sets of `.pose/reports/history` as `recorded-change-sets`, so one published earlier reads as unbound until the next `pose index`.\n  **Children:** a milestone of one local member has no review of its own and stands on that spec's closed approval, and a roadmap may declare `unreviewed_milestones` for milestones whose members are all terminal, sealed by its own review. Otherwise a milestone or roadmap's approval stands only while the review of each child does: a child rejected, stale, with lost attestation history, or not closed under a done parent stops the parent verifying approved, in `review verify`, `review-check` and closeout, and the parent recovers with its child, with no reseal. Selection follows replacement lineage: a replacement of the same content comes before what it replaces whatever the seal times say, a `replaces` naming other content counts for nothing, and a replaced bundle's approval is not retained.\n  **Closeout review policy (schema 5):** an instance that adopts `pose adopt closeout-review-policy` decides with `milestone_review_required` and `roadmap_review_required` (`never`, `when-justified`, `always`) whether the close of a milestone or a roadmap owes its own review. `when-justified` requires it for a milestone with more than two distinct specs, and for a roadmap with more than one distinct spec and at least one ADR association (`adrs: [adr:<file>.md]` on the roadmap, `roadmaps: [roadmap:<slug>]` on the ADR, or a local Markdown link either way, read as CommonMark reads it). `always` requires it even for a one-member milestone, and neither the singleton inheritance nor `unreviewed_milestones` weakens it. Member reviews stay required; a review recorded on the scope anyway is verified as before and its rejection stands; a member or association that cannot be resolved never grants an exemption. A milestone that closes without its own review keeps its exit gate: the gate is carried into the review plan of its only member, or of its last member (the one sealed once every other member is terminal), and the close is blocked until an approved member review judged the gate as it reads now, or the milestone is reviewed itself. A member a transfer moved counts at its destination, and a milestone or roadmap named by `after:`, `consumes:` or a spec's `depends_on` satisfies the edge, and makes a dependent spec ready, only once closed under its project's adopted policy, not by its members' status; a done roadmap closed by its receipt keeps its milestones reachable again if their closure stops verifying. Under the adopted policy `pose close milestone:<roadmap>/<id>` and `pose close roadmap:<slug>` stay explicit in every mode and publish a receipt (`review-closeouts/<milestone|roadmap>/…json` inside `.pose/`) with `review: required|not-required`, the key, the value, `closeout-review@1` and the reason (`members=1, threshold>2`); the scope is terminal only with it. New instances adopt `never`/`never`; an existing policy keeps the legacy rule until it adopts, `pose setup` shows both, `pose doctor` lists open scopes that owe a review, and roadmaps closed before adoption are verified by the contract they were sealed under.\n- `close` — applies a review-gated lifecycle transition to complete a spec, milestone, or roadmap. The transition is published only over the bytes its gates judged, under the spec's lifecycle lock that start and transfer also take (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`): an edit made while the gates ran is a `publish-conflict` that keeps the edit, the authorization is judged again inside the lock, and a start or transfer holding the spec refuses the close with the lock's owner (`start-in-progress` or `spec-lifecycle-in-progress`). Git state (spec `pose-git-and-dispatch-snapshot-integrity`): a `git status`, HEAD or tree that cannot be read is unknown, not clean — the close plan blocks on it rather than sealing evidence over a working tree it could not see (a directory with no repository at all is a different fact and keeps its behaviour); the governed snapshot, the review-bundle path check and the obligation snapshot report an unreadable status as unknown too. `review dispatch` compares the review worktree with the sealed commit's own tree before and after the run, so `assume-unchanged`, `skip-worktree`, a replaced type or a link cannot hide an edit; native derived regeneration requires exact producer outputs that remain regular files; adapter output belongs on stdout or the external output path, and directory names grant no permission to modify the review worktree, and a run that finishes after its bundle was superseded or its policy changed is recorded `stale`, history and not the scope's current fulfilment.\n- `continuous-closeout` — persists and projects continuous closeout terminal state for rapid-iteration workflows. `status --json` writes one operation result whose `data` holds `active` and `terminal`; the exit stays 1 while an active scope is not terminal.\n- **Output channels** — the result (verdict, findings, data) goes to stdout and progress (steps, usage, the command's own failures) to stderr, so `pose x --json | jq` is safe and `2>/dev/null` never hides a finding. `--json` prints one document whose finding `severity` is the untranslated anchor; `--quiet` prints the verdict alone; `pose validate --verbose` streams a check's output instead of capturing it. Colour, symbols and the spinner exist only on a terminal, resolved per stream, and honour `--color`, `POSE_COLOR`, `NO_COLOR` and `TERM`; `COLUMNS` sets the wrap width and `POSE_LOCALE` (`en` or `pt-BR`) the language of every message POSE composes. Exit codes: 0 pass, 1 gate failure, 2 usage error.\n- `artifact-check` — parses exact `### Artifacts` actions, resolves an explicit base/head range or `POSE-Spec: <slug>` commit trailers with safe structured Git arguments, and reports resolvability, existence, action mismatch, undeclared and orphan findings. A claim on a changelog fragment that `pose release prepare` archived resolves through the release manifest, which records the fragment's spec and digest; prepare never edits a spec. A file a later spec removes needs no longer exist for the earlier specs that declare it `created` or `modified`: their claim is satisfied — an edge `removed-by` in `delivery-integrity.json` — when the later spec declares `removed:` for the path, its attributed change sets contain a commit that deleted it (a rename is not a deletion), and every change the earlier spec made to the path — a merge, a root creation, a type change or a deletion included — is a Git ancestor of that commit; a change after the removal, a removal its spec never performed, a change set naming something other than an immutable commit, or history Git cannot read — a shallow clone included; replace refs and grafts are ignored — keeps the existence finding, and the finding says why. Commits modifying artifacts declared by a spec must carry a `POSE-Spec: <slug>` trailer in their commit message for `artifact-check` and `pose close` to attribute the changes. `pose report --change-from/--change-to` persists immutable change-set evidence; `pose index` projects claims, observations, reverse provenance and findings into `delivery-integrity.json`. The seven files `pose index` writes are one generation (spec `pose-index-generation-integrity`): their bytes are written under `generations/<id>/` in the indexes directory, the legacy names are published from the same bytes, and the descriptor `generation.json` there — SHA-256 of each file plus the digests of the inputs they were computed from — is published last and is the commit. A reader fixes the generation the descriptor names and checks each file against it: a set that does not verify (a file missing, replaced, from another generation, or a descriptor it cannot read) is `unavailable`, with the reason, never an empty or mixed result; a repository that never ran `pose index` since this contract keeps working and is reported `legacy-unversioned`, with no claim of coherence. A generated index edited by hand has no effect while the generation's copy exists (readers read the generation), and in a clone without the copy it makes the set unavailable; either way `pose index --verify` names the file. `pose index --verify` reads without writing and exits 1 unless the set is current, the legacy names are those of the generation and its inputs (specs, roadmaps, policy, module metadata, validation matrix) still hold. A failed publication leaves the previous generation in force and a retry converges on the same identity; publishers hold a generation lock in the indexes directory (it never waits: a second `pose index` is refused with `index-generation-busy` and writes nothing, and a lock whose holder is proven dead is recovered), the first publication of a repository is marked pending so one that did not commit reads as `unavailable` and not as a mixture of two runs, an operation that reads several indexes (the review plan) fixes one generation for all of them, and a commit whose durability could not be confirmed is reported as committed; retention keeps the current generation and the newest others up to three, never anything younger than ten minutes, and a retention failure is a warning, not a failed publication. Limits: Git history is read by the delivery graph but not pinned by the descriptor, and a clone without the git-ignored `generations/` directory verifies the legacy names against the descriptor. MCP: `pose_delivery_integrity`.\n- `artifact-backfill` — proposes explicit historical provenance from Git history (`--from-git`) and requires `--confirm-spec-edits` before applying unambiguous proposals to spec artifact claims.\n- `surface-check` / `roadmap-check` — extend the same graph with typed `delivers` refs, production entrypoints, closed validation `evidenceClass` values and provenance-bound structured results. Surface profiles require reachability plus integration/e2e; composed capabilities require integration. Roadmap criteria may reference only registered delivery refs, checks or confined manual-review reports—raw commands are rejected. MCP: `pose_surface_assurance`.\n- `state` (spec `pose-project-state-artifact`) — native project-state artifact: answers \"what is the current state of this project?\" in one read instead of scanning specs/roadmaps/follow-ups/capabilities/knowledge/reports every session. `curated` sections (executive summary, current direction — human prose, preserved verbatim) and `derived` sections (specs & roadmaps, follow-ups, capabilities, decisions & knowledge, validation & evidence, architecture — counts and typed pointers, never copied content). `init` scaffolds; `refresh` recomputes derived sections while preserving curated ones, stamping `generated_at`/`baseline_commit`, and publishes only over the state it read, so a curated edit made while it renders is a `publish-conflict`, never overwritten; a refresh that published the state and could not append its history says so (`state.published=`, refresh log `result: partial`), and the next refresh appends its own entry, while `pose state` reports it as `history-not-appended` (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`); `--if-stale` only redoes the work when the artifact is already `stale` (cheap to run on every CI build). With no subcommand, validates schema, staleness (age/commits since the last refresh, configurable policy), **per-section hash** — a hand-edited derived section fails nominally (`[TAMPERED]`) — and `refresh_pending` (see below). `diff` compares the last two refreshes. MCP equivalent: `pose_project_state` (`section` param fetches just one). Additive: a project without the artifact generated yet stays valid everywhere. The Architecture section reports `unavailable` in this version — no local GraphForge export producer exists yet.\n- `state --governance [--scope <ref>] [--json]` (spec `pose-effective-governance-projection`) — live, read-only projection of what governance is in force here: for each registry contract, optional capability (atomic start, contract nodes, causality closeout, qualified references, spec transfer, criterion reuse, signed attestations, verified identity) and gate (Definition of Ready, delivery integrity), whether it is `supported`, `configured`, `applicable` and `effective`, with reason codes such as `not-adopted`, `no-readiness-cutoff`, `legacy-cutoff:<date>` or `identity-assurance-declared`. `--scope` compares the scope's newest sealed bundle — the contracts it stamped — with what the policy seals today. It reads the same functions the gates call and writes nothing. `pose_project_state` carries the same projection as `effective_governance` (optional `governance_scope`).\n- `state --attention` (specs `pose-obligation-projection`, `pose-state-attention`) — live, read-only answer to \"what is still owed, by whom, restricting which phase?\". Obligations are projected from the subsystems that own them — readiness (unmet `depends_on`, Definition of Ready, unexplained `blocked`), closeout and review of in-progress specs (an unapproved review, each judgment criterion a sealed bundle still owes, text blockers carried as `legacy-opaque`), start reconciliation when atomic start is adopted, and open follow-ups as advisory residual debt — never stored a second time. Project-level sources are projected too (spec `pose-attention-projects-every-source`), originating on `xref:<project>/project:<project>`: an open docs review mark (owed by the doc's owner until `pose docs-review resolve`), a capability mechanism with a stale trigger, a release newer than the newest verified one that is not yet verified, and an accepted-risk or follow-up finding past its review date in the latest legacy review record. Free-form investigation notes carry no state and are not a source. The render shows coverage first: a producer that failed, or action requests until adopted, makes the answer `INCOMPLETE`, so an empty group never reads as nothing owed, and a fresh install, once committed, reads `complete`. Then what needs `--actor` (or any person or role), then what restricts each phase (`start`, `execution`, `review`, `closeout`, `release`), then residual debt. `--json` returns the report — stable `obl-` ids, qualified refs, reason codes, conditions, recipients, effects, satisfaction, knowledge, waiting, a snapshot of revision/dirty tree/policy/contract/index digests, per-producer coverage — plus the `attention` grouping. MCP: `pose_obligations` returns the same ids from the same function. Attention is not a gate.\n- Governance read views (spec `pose-governance-read-view-generation`) — inspect `snapshot.read_view` in `state --attention --json` and `pose_obligations`: its own `schema_version: 1`, observed-input ID, engine/parser versions and root bindings describe one operation. The enclosing report stays at schema version 1. Review and closeout JSON expose the same contract in `read_view`. The ID binds consumed bytes, directory observations, errors and absences; revision, dirty state, size and mtime alone do not prove equivalence. `current` describes the final audit instant only. Coherence, producer coverage and freshness are separate: `unknown` or incomplete coverage must never be read as nothing owed or as write authorization. A historical report without this binding remains readable and proves no current authorization.\n  Reads retain at most 40,000 observations including retained directory entries, 512 MiB of retained input bytes, 8 GiB of streamed bytes (a Git object read whose bytes are only hashed is streamed, keeping its digest) and 16 MiB of metadata. A detected interleaving discards that attempt and retries the whole read once; another change leaves the affected coverage incomplete and freshness `unknown`. Unsupported readers and unbound federated inputs remain unknown. Participating writers acquire authorized root guards before reevaluation and hold them through publication; destination CAS and index descriptor checks remain in force. Writers outside that protocol, including an external change restored before audit, are outside this exclusion guarantee. No watcher, daemon or cross-operation cache is introduced.\n  The global review-closeout check composes an audited selection/catalogue coordinator with independently bounded scope operations. A scope and its recursive children use one closure; workers share only the explicitly bound immutable catalogue. At most 16 scope closures are live, further limited by `POSE_CHECK_WORKERS` or the core count. Scope findings are buffered until their audits and the coordinator audit finish. This proves coherent selection and individually audited results, not a simultaneous snapshot across every completed scope; one exhausted scope cannot consume its sibling's capture budget, and any unresolved loss still fails the global gate.\n- `action <open|list|show|resolve|cancel|waive|invalidate>` (specs `pose-action-requests`, `pose-action-request-resolution`) — material requests to a person or an external system that no other source records: a decision, an approval, an input, an external operation or an acceptance, with its question, options and consequences, recommendation, recipient (principal, role or `unassigned`), qualified targets (`requirement:R4` inside the origin spec, or `xref:…`), per-phase effects (`--effect closeout:block` lets implementation continue) and an optional subject (`--subject requirement:R4` or `path:<file>`) whose change invalidates an answer recorded for the old content. Open one only when a different answer would materially change execution, scope, authority, risk acceptance, closeout or publication and no authorization already given covers it. Each request is an append-only journal under `.pose/actions/`, read whole: a line with a second value, a duplicate field (keys differing only in case included), an unknown event type, another schema, a sequence gap, an empty line or no terminator is never part of a view; `action show|list` and MCP locate it (line, byte offset, length, SHA-256) as an `interrupted-tail`, with a truncation proposal for a person to review, or as `corrupt`, to restore from version control — the engine repairs nothing (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`); `open` is a preview until `--apply`. Targets are qualified with the current project id; when it is derived from the directory name (no `.pose/project.json`, `POSE_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID` or `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` binding), `open --apply` says so (on stderr with `--json`), because a checkout under another name would record another project, and the obligation snapshot lists the same limitation. A resolution names the `--request-digest` it answers and the `--expected-revision` it read, carries an `--idempotency-key` (a retry is a no-op, the same key with other content is refused, a second answer against the same revision is refused) and comes from an actor holding the recipient role in `.pose/policy/actions.json` (`{\"schema_version\":1,\"roles\":{\"maintainer\":[\"human:<name>\"]},\"identity_assurance\":\"declared|verified\"}`); under `verified` it needs `--claim <file>` with an Ed25519 claim from an issuer pinned in the review policy, bound to project, audience, request digest, principal and answer (a human principal also needs `human_authority_issuers`). Answered is not satisfied: a declined approval, a rejected acceptance or a failed external operation leaves the condition unmet. The requester may cancel its own question; a waiver needs the recipient authority and a reason. Open requests are projected as `actor-action` obligations in `state --attention` and `pose_obligations`; MCP opens requests with `pose_action_open` (risk class `governance-write`, a preview unless `apply` is true) and reads them with `pose_action_requests`; `pose_action_resolve` (spec `pose-mcp-action-resolve-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an answer only with the principal's proof — a signature by a key registered to it, or a trusted issuer's claim — and refuses a declared answer under any assurance; its preview returns the statement to sign and the `ssh-keygen` command.\n- Action destination scoping (spec `pose-action-target-and-node-scoping`) — origin is provenance, targets select relevance, and effects select restrictions. An empty effect scope still means the whole source artifact; a roadmap or milestone expands its current members. Explicit scopes replace that default, so a request from alpha can restrict beta without blocking alpha. A named whole artifact is a whole restriction, not partial merely because it is qualified. Pending requests remain visible when their origin becomes terminal. Unknown membership or unreadable material is reported as limited coverage and never expands into every spec. CLI accepts `--effect-json '{\"phase\":\"start\",\"mode\":\"block\",\"scope\":[\"xref:project/spec:beta\"]}'`; MCP `effects` accepts the same object alongside legacy `\"start:block\"` strings. Targets and subjects resolve by project/artifact/kind/ID, and a subject is re-read in its recorded artifact. Under adopted agency readiness, new approvals and acceptances require an explicit `--subject` / `subject`; legacy unbound requests retain their journal and report that answers have no content-change detection. Policy-off creation and transition defaults remain unchanged. This compatibility tightening requires release review; no policy adoption happens here.\n- Signed answers (spec `pose-signed-action-answers`) — a principal proves an answer with an SSH key the project registered, with no external service. `pose identity add [<principal>] --key <file.pub> [--role <role>] --apply` records the key under `keys` in `.pose/policy/actions.json` (only `ssh-ed25519` and `sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com`; a key belongs to one principal; a private key file is refused); without a principal it suggests `human:<name>` from the git identity, which is a name, not a proof. `pose identity list [--json]` shows principals, roles and fingerprints; `pose identity remove <principal> [--fingerprint <fp>] --apply` stops future answers by a key without touching roles or answers already recorded. `pose action resolve … --sign <private key>` signs with the user's own `ssh-keygen -Y sign -n pose-action-answer` (POSE never reads the key); `--signature <file>` attaches a signature made elsewhere over the exact bytes `pose action statement <act-id> --actor <p> --answer <a> --idempotency-key <k>` prints, which bind project, request id and digest, actor, answer and idempotency key. POSE verifies the SSHSIG natively, records statement, signature and key on the answer, and `pose action show` verifies it again from the journal. A signed answer is `verified` under any assurance; under `identity_assurance: verified` an answer needs a registered key's signature or a trusted issuer's claim — two emitters of identity proof, Harne8 being one issuer. Presence follows the explicit channel contract below. `pose doctor` (`actions.keys`) warns when a human role holder under verified assurance can prove no answer, or signs only with keys that cannot prove presence (an error under `require_presence`).\n- Policy scope validation (spec `pose-policy-scope-validation`) — `identity_assurance` and `reviewer_independence` accept only exact `spec`, `milestone` and `roadmap` keys in every supported review-policy schema, including disabled configuration. Empty keys, typos, whitespace, case variants and future kinds are refused with the field, quoted key and supported kinds; no key is normalized and no operator file is repaired automatically. Unknown top-level metadata remains permitted. This stricter refusal is breaking and requires major-release classification, without automatic migration or adoption. Current preparation, `state --governance` and MCP `pose_project_state` report invalid policy explicitly; no partial projection advertises protection. A protected Git baseline with an invalid selector stops new preparation instead of falling back to an unprotected warning. Global governance entries describe instance configuration and name the protected kinds; `--scope`/`governance_scope` adds historical bundle context, not per-scope capability evaluation. Malformed context selectors are refused. Historical sealed records and their stamped gates remain readable and unchanged; inability to evaluate current policy is not a retrospective invalidation of those records.\n- Human presence (spec `pose-proof-presence-channel-parity`) — preserve roles and keys when explicitly migrating `.pose/policy/actions.json` to `schema_version: 2`. Presence defaults to disabled; when `require_presence: true`, choose `presence_scope: \"universal\"` or `\"sshsig\"`. Universal requires a registered SK signature with an authenticated user-present assertion for every human answer, including issuer-backed answers. SSHSIG scope requires it when an SSH signature is supplied; issuer-only answers are explicitly outside that scope and establish no presence. An issuer claim may accompany an SK signature, but every supplied proof must verify. Identity grants, Portal authentication and user-verification alone are not presence. Agents do not acquire human-presence assurance. Enabled schema-1 policy requires migration before new human answers; unknown scopes are refused and older engines reject schema 2. This is a breaking change, requiring major-release classification, without automatic policy adoption. Previews disclose current policy as not-evaluated and never request a touch. Historical reads reverify recorded proof, disclose the key's assertion separately from identity, and leave historical presence requirements unrecorded; they never impose today's scope retrospectively. A verified assertion does not attest hardware provenance or who touched the key.\n- Guided setup (spec `pose-setup-command`) — `pose setup` shows, in one place, the project identity, principals and keys (with the principal the git identity suggests, which is a name and not a proof), the pre-commit gate, capabilities in force, capabilities new since the last configuration review, what needs setup, whether the configuration is committed, and one next step with its command. At a terminal it offers each open step — the hook, registering a key with the `maintainer` role, each new capability (adopt, decline or defer with a reason) — and performs it only after an explicit yes; with `--json`, `--no-input` or no terminal it changes nothing. New means off, undecided and introduced after `reviewed_version` in `.pose/policy/adoption-decisions.json` (every off, undecided capability when none is recorded); a fresh install records its engine version, deciding the last new capability advances it, and a deferral is asked again under a newer engine. `pose install` ends with `pose setup` as the next step, `pose update` names the pending decisions instead of \"Nothing to do\", and `pose doctor` reports them as a `next` step (`setup.capabilities`).\n- Onboarding spec (spec `pose-onboarding-spec`) — a new instance starts with a governed first spec, `.pose/specs/<date>-pose-onboarding.md` (draft, in the install's locale): adopting POSE is the project's first piece of work, with requirements for the declared identity, a maintainer able to prove answers, the commit gate, the reviewed capability defaults and the committed instance, tasks that point to `pose setup`, a decision recording the defaults, and checks (`pose doctor`, `pose check --strict`). It passes lint, the strict check and the Definition of Ready as written. `pose setup` names `pose start spec:pose-onboarding` first and, once every other step is done, `pose close spec:pose-onboarding`. Only an install that creates the instance writes it; `pose update` and a reinstall never create or overwrite it.\n- Configuration review (spec `pose-update-configuration-review`) — when `pose update` finds capabilities this project has not decided for the engine it runs, it writes `.pose/specs/<date>-pose-configuration-review-<version>.md` (draft, one requirement per capability with its effect, version and recommendation) and opens one decision request per capability (`adopt`, `decline`, `defer`; `adopt` recommended for a capability new instances get), addressed to the `maintainer` role and restricting only the review spec's closeout. Nothing is adopted by the update. The maintainer answers with `pose action resolve` (signed when the policy asks, or over MCP with `pose_action_resolve`) or at a `pose setup` prompt; `pose adopt --request <act-id> [--apply]` applies an answered request — adopt turns the capability on dated that day, decline and defer record the decision with the answer's reason and the request id — and refuses an open, cancelled or foreign request. A second update for the same version asks nothing again; `pose setup` lists open review requests with the resolve command and answered ones not yet applied.\n- Phase readiness (spec `pose-phase-scoped-readiness`) — `pose_spec_readiness` with `phases: true`, and `state --attention --scope spec:<slug>`, report per phase (`start`, `execution`, `review`, `closeout`, `release`) whether it is `clear`, `restricted`, `partially-restricted` on named nodes or `unknown`. A phase is `clear` only when no obligation restricts it and every producer that could restrict it was read; a restriction on `R4` alone is reported as partial with the note that independence of the rest is not demonstrated. `ready` keeps its legacy meaning: partial eligibility never turns it true.\n- Governed capabilities (spec `pose-governed-capabilities-default-on-new-instances`) — agency readiness, contract nodes, atomic start and causality closeout with the `structural-materiality@1` overlay. A new instance adopts all four: when `pose install` creates `.pose/policy/review.json` it sets their keys, dates atomic start, causality closeout and the overlay with the install day, and logs each adoption. An instance that already has a review policy adopts nothing at install, and `pose update` never adopts, even when it seeds a missing policy. `pose adopt <capability>` previews the keys one capability needs and `--apply` writes them, dated today or `--date YYYY-MM-DD`, so work created before the date is not re-judged; adopting an adopted capability keeps its date, `--off` removes the same keys, and a result the review-policy reader would refuse is never written. `state --governance` names the command for each capability not adopted. Every adoptable capability is in one catalog (spec `pose-capability-catalog`): the four above, criterion reuse, the Definition of Ready, the review overlays (`overlay:<profile>`), qualified references and spec authority transfer (which raise `schema_version` as their contract requires), signed attestations and verified identity. `pose adopt --list` shows each with its state — `on`, `off`, `declined`, `deferred` or `needs-setup` —, what it changes day to day, since which version and whether a new instance gets it (`--json` for the same data); a new instance adopts every capability the catalog marks as a default, the Definition of Ready and criterion reuse included. A capability whose requirement is off, or whose prerequisite (a trusted issuer, an authority audience) is missing, is refused with what is missing; `--off` is refused while another adopted capability depends on it. `pose adopt <capability> --decline --reason <text>` or `--defer --reason <text>` records in `.pose/policy/adoption-decisions.json` that the project looked and chose not to adopt it now, so it is not asked again; adopting later clears it, and the file is never shipped to another instance. A new instance's role map in `.pose/policy/actions.json` names nobody, and `pose doctor` lists naming them as a next step. A fresh install reports no warning in `pose doctor`: milestone and roadmap profiles count only once the project has a roadmap, and evidence-class coverage reads only the required checks of the shipped stacks some module uses (every shipped test and build check declares its class).\n- Governed effects (spec `pose-governed-effect-enforcement`) — opt-in with `agency_readiness_version: 1` in `.pose/policy/review.json`. Once adopted, an unsatisfied action request refuses the transition it restricts at its write point: `pose close` refuses with `close.refused.action-request-pending` and the request's message, `closeout-check` / `pose_closeout_state` report the typed diagnostic and `next_step: answer-action-request`, continuous closeout cannot complete, `pose start --apply` refuses a plan restricted at start (a preview taken before a request was opened is stale at apply), and `pose release prepare` refuses — dry-run included — while a request restricts release of one of the release's specs. Each gate recomputes the restriction when it runs; a declined approval keeps refusing. Only action requests are enforced here: dependencies, review and evidence keep their own gates. Without adoption, requests are shown in Attention and restrict nothing, and `state --governance` reports the capability as supported and not adopted.\n- Spec transfer and action requests (spec `pose-transfer-preserves-obligations`) — `spec-transfer preview` lists the source spec's action requests in `action_requests` with a disposition: `invalidate-in-source` for an open or answered request, `history-only` for one already closed. When the source is retired, each open or answered request receives an invalidation event keyed to the operation, so a resumed transfer records it once; a request opened after the preview makes the plan stale. Requests and answers are bound to their project and grant nothing in the destination, which re-opens a request if it still applies; a same slug and requirement id in two projects never share a request.\n- `close spec:<slug> --plan|--apply|--resume` (spec `pose-recoverable-closeout-plan`) — the closeout as an ordered, recoverable plan computed from the repository: context (an in-progress spec, no uncommitted change outside the paths the plan writes), evidence and index (validate into `results_path`, then `pose index`, as a pair), seal, mechanical attestation (`--reviewer` names the reviewing execution; never invented), judgment, verify and the guarded transition. `--plan` previews (`--json` too); `--apply [--digest <plan digest>]` refuses a plan that no longer matches the repository, runs the mechanical steps and stops with exit 3 at a pending judgment, listing it with the sealed evidence refs (`closeout_plan.evidence`) and an attest command filled with them, one `--tool` per required tool and one `--criterion` per pending judgment (`closeout_plan.attest`, spec `pose-quickstart-real-lifecycle`); a requirement trace that is incomplete — a requirement with no entry, an orphan or malformed entry, or requirements with no `### Requirement trace` — blocks the plan at its `trace` step, before anything is sealed, and `pose close` refuses the transition for the same reason, so a closed spec never fails the lint that follows; `--resume` re-plans after an interruption, so done steps are not repeated and no bundle or attestation is duplicated. A checkpoint under `.pose/closeout-plans/` records progress and is removed on completion; a removal that fails is reported. It is progress, never a receipt (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`): `--plan` shows it as `valid`, `corrupt`, `unsupported` or `unavailable`; an apply over one that does not read stops before any step, for a person to inspect and remove it; and one that records an evidence, index, seal, attestation or transition step the repository does not reflect is a conflict, until `--apply --digest <current plan>` starts a new run. The plan never answers a judgment, publishes or edits policy, and promises a recoverable operation, not a transaction over Git and external processes. Operation results (spec `pose-cli-mcp-operation-results`, ADR 2026-10-16): `--apply|--resume --json` writes exactly one JSON document — `operation`, `outcome` (`completed`, `already-satisfied`, `waiting`, `blocked`, `conflict`, `failed`), the `step` it stopped at, `steps_run`, `pending`, `next_action`, the `effects` the domain confirmed, the steps left `unconfirmed` after a failure, `resumable`, a typed `error.cause` and the plan in `data` — and the human output reads the same result. A call that runs no step reports `already-satisfied`, not `closed`: it is not a fresh verification, and `pose closeout-check` is. Two different modes, or `--digest`, `--reviewer` or `--skip-evidence` with `--plan`, exit 2 before anything runs; `pose start` refuses two of `--apply`, `--status` and `--cancel`, `--digest` outside `--apply` and `--cancel --json`; an apply that fails after recording its baseline reports the recorded phase as a confirmed effect and the same `--apply --digest` as its next action, which finishes from the start record a transition already published; a spec that has not moved is judged again by the current plan and its governed restrictions, and a baseline the repository has moved past is reported as a conflict to cancel and preview again, never as resumable; a failed apply lists every state it confirms as an effect (the baseline and, once published, the spec moved to `in-progress`) and in `steps_run` only the steps that call ran, and a start record left unreadable (a baseline write cut short) is reported with the `baseline` step unconfirmed and a next action to inspect and remove it, never repaired automatically. `pose close <scope>` without a plan mode refuses `--json`. An MCP gate (`pose_check`, `pose_lint_spec`, `pose_skills_check`) stopped by its deadline or a cancellation returns `isError` with `error_code: execution_inconclusive`, its cause and partial output, never `passed=false`. The usage journal records a stop on a reviewer as `waiting`, not `failed`, and never averages an unmeasured interval as 0 ms. The plan modes are a spec's: `pose close <scope> --plan|--apply|--resume` on a milestone or a roadmap exits 2 and says so. Any other scope closes with `pose close <scope>` and no plan flag (the guarded transition: it verifies the gate, which on a large repository takes minutes, and names the next closeout, `pose close roadmap:<slug>` once every milestone of a roadmap is closed); `pose closeout-check <scope>` reads whether it is terminal and is what `terminal=true` means for a milestone or a roadmap.\n- Reuse by material equivalence (spec `pose-material-equivalence-reuse`) — a criterion's answer is reused only when every input it depends on is equal in the new bundle: its definition, the independence, the scope sections, the subject entries (all of them for a subject-sensitive criterion), the evidence of the classes it accepts, the governing inputs and its tools; judgment reuse also needs `allow_criterion_reuse`. When a bundle is superseded, `review verify` (`delta.criterion_reuse` in JSON) shows each criterion as `equivalent`, `changed` with the inputs that changed, or `new`, and each reused answer names the attestation it came from. Editing bookkeeping such as the Validation log changes no criterion; a code change names the changed subject.\n- Assessment freshness (spec `pose-adaptive-assessment-freshness`) — a component assessment records its binding: the committed tree of the component, the engine version and the validation-matrix digest. `pose assess discover --if-stale [--component <dir>]` reuses an assessment whose binding is unchanged and refreshes it otherwise, printing `assess.freshness.<component>` with the reason (content changed, engine changed, matrix changed, uncommitted changes, or no binding on an older record). Age alone never stales an assessment. A missing or stale assessment of a component an in-progress spec declares appears as an advisory `evidence` obligation in `state --attention`; it gates nothing.\n- `followups --candidates [--json]` (spec `pose-followup-reconciliation-candidates`) — lists open follow-ups with a reconciliation reason: `target-terminal` (a spec the follow-up names is done, superseded or abandoned), `evidence-present` (a test it names now exists), `overdue` (its review date passed), plus lexical near-duplicate clusters. Each candidate shows its evidence and its limits; nothing is dispositioned, similarity never becomes `duplicate` without judgment, and an open follow-up never leaves Attention without a disposition written in its spec. Attention shows the same reason on the residual-debt item.\n- `action list --present [--actor <id|role>] [--json]` (spec `pose-action-request-presentation`) — groups the requests still waiting for an answer for one conversation: by origin and earliest restricted phase, the ones restricting start or execution first (`interrupt`), release-only ones marked as able to wait. Each request keeps its own id, request digest, revision and answer, and shows exactly the content a resolution binds to; satisfied requests are not asked again. MCP: `pose_action_requests` with `present: true`. Deciding when to interrupt a person is left to the consumer (Harne8).\n- Progressive spec surface (spec `pose-progressive-spec-surface`) — `pose new-spec <slug> --surface minimal|standard|full` records `surface:` in the frontmatter. A `minimal` spec keeps Intent, Requirements, the Technical Plan with its Artifacts, Validation with the requirement trace and the Final Report with its follow-ups; it drops the planner-local Tasks section and the report subsections that `pose specs facts <slug> [--json]` derives from the delivery index (paths of the attributed commits, checks that produced scoped evidence). `lint-spec` stops requiring Tasks for a minimal spec and relaxes nothing else: the trace, follow-up and amendment gates apply as before. The facts never include intent, rationale or accepted risk. Evidence binding (spec `pose-spec-facts-evidence-binding`): `specs facts` separates `checks` (results whose scope provenance names the spec, with their basis and whether they ran against the head of the spec's change sets) from `candidates` (results the index holds that nothing ties to the spec: a declared module or an empty `components` is not provenance); candidates are printed as `CANDIDATE, not evidence of this spec`. `freshness` reports `fresh`, `stale` (an input changed since the index was published), `unknown` (never published as a set) or `unavailable`, and a limitation says to run `pose index` when it cannot be established; reading the facts never rebuilds the index and never concludes that a requirement is satisfied.\n- **Automatic project-state refresh** (spec `pose-project-state-refresh-contract`) — an internal post-event hook registry fires a **partial** `pose state refresh` (only the sections an event affects) at the points POSE already intercepts: a successful `pose lint-spec <slug> --strict` on a `status: done` spec (`spec_closeout` → Specs & Roadmaps, Follow-ups, Validation & Evidence), `pose amend` (`spec_amend` → Specs & Roadmaps, Decisions & Knowledge), `pose reconcile-evidence record` (`evidence_reconciled` → Validation & Evidence) and `pose assess snapshot` (`assessment_snapshot` → Capabilities). When the event carries a commit and `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` is configured, the consumer calls `components_hit` (spec `graphforge-components-hit-contract`) from the state's baseline to the event's commit and attaches the hit components to the Architecture section (**directed** refresh); without GraphForge configured, refresh stays full/undirected — zero build coupling. No daemon, no filesystem watcher: it is a synchronous call at the exact point each event already happens. **Best-effort by default**: a failed refresh never blocks the command that fired the event — it marks `refresh_pending: <event>` in the state frontmatter instead (cleared by the next successful refresh, regardless of trigger). Strict mode is opt-in via policy (`strict_refresh: true`) — there, a refresh failure fails the triggering command. Every run (triggered or manual) is logged to the artifact's own append-only log (metadata only: trigger, target, result `ok|failed|skipped`, duration, changed-section hashes — never content). Dedup key `hash(event+target+commit)`: the same event processed twice (retry/replay) does not repeat the refresh, result `skipped`; `manual`/`ci` refreshes are never deduped against each other (an explicit call always runs). `release_cut` is registered in the event→sections map but has no producer inside pose-mcp today — cutting a release belongs to Conductor (a separate service); the trigger is ready for when that integration exists.\n- **Capability reassessment triggers** (spec `pose-capability-assessment-triggers`, same hook registry as the automatic project-state refresh above) — the `assessment-staleness` consumer, registered on `spec_closeout`, resolves which components a closeout reached (via `components_hit` when `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` is configured; fallback: intersecting the event's touched files against each mechanism's manually declared `paths:` globs in the assessment) and marks every affected capability mechanism stale — never mutating a score, only recording `since`/`trigger`/`hits` and projecting a cobrável demand (origin `assessment-trigger`) into `pose followups --open`, with no duplicate storage. `pose assess snapshot` clears the marks on the mechanisms it reassesses and records the mark→snapshot link in the history. On-demand: `pose assess stale [--json]` lists pending marks; `pose assess request --mechanism <id> [--reason <text>]` creates one manually (the same path a UI-driven \"flag for reassessment\" action would call over MCP). MCP tool: `pose_capability_stale`. Without GraphForge and without `paths:` declared, the event logs `capability_mapping_unavailable` to the refresh log — a visible signal, nothing marked silently. Anti-noise threshold (`min_hits`, hit `level` `direct`/`any`, the demand's default owner/SLA) is configurable, sharing the same policy file already used for age/commit-based staleness (optional; absent means these same conservative defaults).\n- **Docs governance** (spec `pose-docs-governance-contract`) — an opt-in, per-project contract for documentation, same mechanic as the capability assessment above: absent, every project stays valid everywhere. `pose docs-init [--profile library|service|cli|monorepo]` scaffolds a manifest declaring governed doc roots and, per doc, `path`/`doc_type` (Diátaxis `tutorial`/`howto`/`reference`/`explanation`, or a custom value)/`topics`/`owns`/`applies_to`/optional `review_after`. `pose docs-check [--json] [--explain <rule>]` runs seven deterministic, offline rules — a declared doc missing from disk, a file present but undeclared, missing minimal frontmatter (`title`/`doc_type`), a broken relative link, a broken typed reference (`spec:`/`adr:`/`knowledge:`/`doc:`/...), staleness (an entry's own review date, or the manifest's default review window counted from its last touching commit) and a security scan reusing the same deterministic unsafe-instruction/secret-shaped pattern check skills already run — each with a configurable severity (`error`/`warning`/`off`). `pose check --strict` incorporates `docs-check` when the manifest exists (opt-in by presence); only errors block, warnings surface without blocking. `pose docs-sync` synchronizes governance metadata. Tool MCP: `pose_docs_state`. Project-state gains an additive Docs section (manifest presence/profile/roots, declared/undeclared/stale/error/warning counts).\n- **Docs review-pending triggers** (spec `pose-docs-assessment-followups`, third consumer of the same hook registry as the two entries above — reused unmodified) — a `docs-review` consumer, registered on `spec_closeout`, resolves which components/files a closeout reached (`components_hit` when configured, matched against `owns:` entries declared as `component:<id>`; otherwise the event's touched files matched against each doc's `owns:` paths/globs, where a directory like `\"site\"` covers every file under it) and marks every doc whose declared area was reached as review-pending — never editing the doc itself, never touching a score. Marks accumulate in an append-only log outside the doc's own file and project a synthetic, owned demand into `pose followups --open` (origin `docs:<doc-path>`), reusing the manifest entry's own `owner` field when declared. `pose docs-review resolve <doc> [--no-change --reason <text>] [--commit <sha>]` closes every mark pending on a doc at once, recording `updated` (default, captures the current commit) or `no_change_needed` (reason required); `pose docs-review request <doc>`/`--all-stale` cover the on-demand path — the latter bridges every currently-stale doc into an active demand in one call. `docs-check`'s own output and `pose_docs_state` additively list what's still pending. Without a component map and without `owns:` declared, the event logs a visible signal instead of marking anything silently — here the path fallback is the mechanism's full-strength path (`owns:` is expressed as paths by default), not a lesser one. Anti-noise threshold (`min_hits`, hit `level`, default owner/SLA) is configurable, sharing the same policy shape as the capability triggers in its own file (optional; absent means these same conservative defaults).\n- `history-check` — verifies every `.jsonl` under `reports/history/` is git-tracked. Without that, `recurrence-check` and `stats` diverge between machines. Strict blocks; tolerant warns.\n- `suggest` — reads [`task-map.json`](.pose/indexes/task-map.json) and prints the canonical trail (workflow + skill + rules + spec/ADR + knowledge) for a task type. With no arguments it lists every type. `--domain <d>` adds domain rules (frontend, backend-go, k8s); `--path <p>` infers the domain heuristically and via [`repo-map.json`](.pose/indexes/repo-map.json) (`language` → frontend/backend-go); `--json` for agents. Domains (spec `pose-distributed-instructions-parity`): `--domain` and the domain inferred from `--path` go through one taxonomy (`go`/`golang`/`backend-go` → `backend-go`; `react`/`frontend-react`/`frontend` → `frontend`; `kubernetes`/`helm`/`k8s` → `k8s`), the JSON discloses `domain_input` and `domain_source: alias`, and a domain the task map has no rules for is reported (`domain_status`) instead of borrowing another's; a `backend` module is the Go domain only when its language is Go. `rules_status` (and the human output) says for each rule whether it is `installed` (with its path), `extension-needed` (with `pose extension install <id>`) or `unavailable`; suggest announces no rule file that is not on disk and installs nothing.\n- `stats` — aggregates history JSONL outcomes by workflow, task or context. Enables objective decisions (promoting a check from optional to required, spotting unstable workflows, comparing ci against manual). `--since-days N` filters the window; `--json` for machine consumption.\n- `stats governance` — schema 2 read-only projection over report history, sealed review bundles, attestations and explicit remediation declarations. It keeps preparation, judgment, intervention, freshness, telemetry, provenance and coverage independent, never emits a quality score, and reports missing/invalid data explicitly. `--report-type` filters validation attempts; `--band` filters reviews and finding links. Replay identity is `(report_type, task_slug, sequence)`; identity-less rows remain visible in coverage. MCP `pose_governance_stats` accepts the same selectors and requires `project_id` when choosing a federated root. Explicit finding links contain only opaque IDs, spec slugs, category and source band, with at most 100 rows and truncation reported.\n- `stats governance --waits --rework` (spec `pose-governance-wait-rework-observability`) — two more dimensions beside the outcomes, never folded into a score. With `--waits` or `--rework` only the requested dimensions are computed; `--outcomes` adds the outcomes, which read the whole history. `--waits` times action requests and keeps three measures apart: the age of each request, the interval attributed to waiting on an actor or an external system, and `known_blocking_seconds`, the union per spec of intervals in which a request restricted the whole spec's execution (simultaneous requests count once). None of them is inactivity; missing timestamps are counted as unknown. `--rework` classifies superseded sealed bundles by what changed (subject, intent or plan, review plan, evidence, or unknown) and extra attestations on one bundle by review disagreement or an earlier attestation invalid against today's policy, leaving other causes unknown. `scripts/bench-governance.sh [runs] [pose-binary]` times `state --attention`, `check --strict` and `index` on the same revision and reports time and the index size separately, with the corpus revision and engine version.\n- Premise validity (spec `pose-assumption-validity-scope`) — a material `Assumption A<N>` may declare `Valid scope:` (the context it holds in) and `Stale trigger:` entries `<kind>:<value>@<pin>`, where kind is `doc`, `report`, `knowledge`, `adr` or `snapshot` and the pin is a prefix (8+ hex) of the sha256 of the content the premise was judged against. `lint-spec --design-check` reports an unpinned trigger with the pin to append, an unreadable one as unknown, and a calendar trigger (`date:`, `ttl:`, …) as not evaluated: validity is by context, not expiry, and trivial premises need neither field. When pinned content changes, the obligation projection (producer `premises`) emits a `premise-stale` judgment obligation for draft and in-progress specs, advisory on review and scoped to the assumption and the decisions based on it. The recorded evidence stays; it is described as judged against the pinned content and not valid for the current one, and nothing is invalidated automatically. Provider or remote contracts are not monitored by the core. Trigger state is projection only and never changes the design digest.\n- Falsifier observation (spec `pose-falsifier-reconsideration`) — a material `Decision D<N>` may add `Expected effect:` and `Falsifier check: check:<module>/<check>`, naming the indexed validation check whose failure contradicts it. When the delivery index holds a `fail` result for that check, the obligation projection (producer `falsifiers`) emits a `falsifier-observed` judgment obligation, advisory on review and scoped to the decision, for any spec that is not superseded or abandoned (decisions stay in force after done). The engine infers no cause and judges no architecture: the failure is an observation, the decision and its rationale are never edited, and a person either records why it still holds or opens a superseding decision. A passing or absent result raises nothing. `lint-spec --design-check` warns on a malformed reference and on a falsifier check without a stated falsifier.\n- `migrate v7 --dry-run [--json]` (spec `pose-v7-legacy-cleanup-plan`) — read-only inventory of what a future major would convert, keep or refuse: spec layouts, `blocked` specs, legacy and unknown review policy keys, sealed bundles, attestations by identity mode and attribution, and incomplete transfers on this project's side, each with its action and removal criterion. It writes nothing. Sealed bundles, attestations and signatures are never rewritten. `--apply` is refused with the current fact: no apply is available in this engine, the only mode is `--dry-run`, which writes nothing; no future apply is promised.\n- `assess design --spec <slug>` — read-only bounded projection over the canonical sealed review subject. It observes dependency, component, delivery-metadata, governance-contract and public-contract deltas, keeps unknown/unsupported coverage explicit, and never computes a complexity or architecture score. The MCP equivalent is `pose_design_delta`.\n- `usage` — aggregates automatic, project-scoped CLI and MCP usage events: calls, execution/semantic outcomes, latency, findings observed and stable finding lifecycle (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agents never maintain counters. Events are best-effort, local-only and stored outside the tracked worktree; raw arguments, output, paths, project/user identity and finding IDs are never persisted. `--since-days 0` includes all history; filter with `--tool` or `--surface`; MCP equivalent: `pose_usage`. This is product-usage evidence, not a DORA delivery outcome or an individual productivity score.\n- `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingest identity-free schema-v2 delivery events and calculate metrics for explicit production environments (`--environment`, default `production`). Deployments require `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidents require an environment; recovery includes only resolved `--caused-by-deployment` incidents. `adoption-metrics` aggregates specifications, roadmaps, and validation lifecycle rates.\n- `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — read-only, offline catalog inspection (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Matches directory entries against the maintained profile catalog (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — and **.NET**), reporting per profile: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (multiple managers present resolve by declared priority), `confidence` (`medium` under conflict), and whether the prerequisite native tool is on `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, never by executing a project file. Detected markers feed `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` the same way Node/Go/Rust/Java already do; the actual checks run through [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json) `python`/`dotnet` stacks. Python manager priority is expressed with `when.fileNotExistsAny` (skip when any higher-priority lockfile/marker exists) alongside the existing `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` predicates.\n- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle.\n- `doctor [--fix]` — environment, dependency, native runtime, and POSE instance health diagnostics, plus governance that would otherwise fail only downstream: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (a selected profile demands a class no check may emit), `validate.class-producers` (a criterion accepts only classes no registered check produces), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks with no `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set`, and `<policy>.policy-keys` for nine policies (keys the engine does not read, i.e. a misspelled setting that silently does nothing).\n- `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — idempotent maintenance (list/archive/purge). Mutations require `--apply`. Reports housekeeping **never touches `history/`**: the JSONL is the source of truth for `recurrence-check` and `report`'s temporal comparisons. Defaults: stale = 120d, archive purge = 365d.\n- `amend` — append-only spec amendment history (`amendments.jsonl` beside a folder spec's `spec.md`, or `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` beside a flat dated spec; the spec is resolved through the store, so both layouts amend alike). `--baseline` snapshots every R-ID hash; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` acknowledges a material change; `--list` renders history and pending acknowledgments. On `done` specs with a history, `lint-spec` rejects any requirement whose current text is not acknowledged by an event — specs cannot be silently rewritten after evidence. `--nodes [--json]` prints the versioned contract-node projection: requirements, assumptions and decisions with namespace, content hash, state and relations. With `contract_nodes_version: 1` adopted in the review policy, `--baseline` writes a schema-2 R/A/D snapshot, `--ids` accepts A/D, `--change transition` acknowledges an allowed state change, `editorial` is refused when a node changed state or relations, and the gate also rejects an active decision resting on an invalidated or withdrawn assumption. Without the capability the gate stays requirement-only and a schema-2 event is refused; schema-1 logs keep their meaning.\n- `start` — atomic start of a draft spec, journaled in a per-spec start record. Without flags it is a read-only, digest-bound preview: readiness and dependencies, the declared review obligations (no future structural delta needed) and the R/A/D baseline. `--apply --digest <sha256>` records that baseline and moves the spec from `draft` to `in-progress` under a per-spec lock with compare-and-swap; it needs `atomic_start_version: 1` in the review policy, repeats idempotently, resumes an interrupted run and refuses another plan for a started spec. `--cancel` undoes a start that never moved the spec. The per-spec lock records its owner (host, digests of the machine and boot IDs, PID namespace, PID and process start); a lock another call holds is reported, never broken by age, as `owner-active`, `owner-unknown` or `owner-dead` (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`). Dead is proven only on the same Linux machine — another boot, or a PID that is not running or now belongs to another process — and `--recover-lock <owner digest>`, with the digest `--status` shows, removes that lock only if its bytes and its dead owner are unchanged, leaving the start record to resume; an unknown owner (another host, an older engine's empty lock, another platform) is checked and removed by a person. The same lock is the spec's lifecycle lock: close and transfer take it too, so no two of them interleave on one spec (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`). `--status` classifies each node as `recorded-before-managed-execution`, `introduced-during-execution` or `legacy-unbaselined`, recomputes obligations and, under the capability, asks for reconciliation when status or the recorded baseline was edited by hand. `atomic_start_adopted_at: YYYY-MM-DD` (spec `pose-atomic-start-adoption-cutoff`) limits the capability to specs created on or after that date: an older spec in progress without a start is reported as `legacy-unbaselined` with a note and blocks nothing; without the date every such spec needs reconciliation. A start records observable precedence, not when anything was decided; MCP exposes the read-only view as `pose_start_status`.\n- `knowledge-usage` — projects `knowledge:<slug>` citations from specs per artifact (owner, expiry, citing specs). Usage signals inform the owner's review; TTL is never extended automatically. Dangling `knowledge:` refs fail `knowledge-check`.\n- `knowledge-suggest <query>` / `semantic-suggest <query>` / `suggest-feedback` / `portfolio-projection` / `reconcile-evidence` — deterministic lexical ranking and advisory projections for knowledge, feedback clustering, multi-roadmap portfolio milestones, and delivery validation evidence reconciliation. Suggestions never gate or auto-apply without confirmation.\n- `hooks` — links the native binary into `.git/hooks/`; invocation name selects `check --tolerant` for `pre-commit` and `index` for `post-merge`. `install --force` keeps a backup of pre-existing hooks. An instance that runs POSE through its own revision-bound CLI declares a runner in a `hooks.json` file of `.pose/policy/` (`{\"schema_version\":1,\"runner\":\"<project-relative executable>\"}`): the hook then runs the runner with the hook's name and returns its exit code, and `hooks status` names it; a runner outside the project, not executable or under another schema is refused and nothing runs.\n- `version` — displays compiled Go binary version, commit SHA, and compares the repository schema version with engine requirements.\n- `context` — resolves a selected logical project and optional local or `xref:`-qualified task through the shared artifact resolver. The JSON result includes canonical authority, project/artifact revisions, resolution state, supported contracts and a `context_revision`; it never includes filesystem roots. Use this token to detect a changed checkout or revision before a cross-project write.\n- Cross-project CLI writes require the target in `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` and the `context_revision` from a fresh `pose context` call. Use `new-spec-qualified` for qualified creation so older engines reject the command itself. A project found only by `HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` scan can be read but does not grant write access. Existing canonical specs are reused; redirects and incomplete transfers block shadow creation.\n- `install <dir> [--locale tag] [--skip-mcp] [--force] [--no-backup] [--allow-non-git]` — installs the embedded POSE runtime, rules, workflows, templates, and documentation into a target project directory without cloning, stamps `adopted_at` in the changelog policy with the day the instance received it, and runs the strict gate.\n- `import <spec-kit|openspec> <path> [--dry-run]` — imports foreign specification trees into canonical POSE specifications with standard frontmatter and section structure.\n- `serve-mcp` — starts the POSE Model Context Protocol server over stdio (`--stdio`) or HTTP for native AI agent integration. The stdio server exits on SIGTERM; it resolves the `pose` CLI it runs once, at start, so a server started before `pose update` keeps working with the updated binary.\n- `report-limitation` — records an engine limitation, defect, or enhancement proposal into `.pose/feedback/` with optional submission (`--submit`).\n- `contribute <enable|disable|status|stage|list>` — open-source contributor mode; stages sanitized reports under `.pose/contributions/` without leaking proprietary code; submission remains developer-adjudicated.\n- `telemetry <enable|disable|status>` — manages anonymous, opt-in platform telemetry settings.\n- `release` / `release-notes` — governed release lifecycle (`plan`, `prepare`, `check`, `notes`, `record`, `status`, `open-next`, `backfill`) ensuring immutable release notes, manifest freezing, provider evidence importing, and reproducible release verification. A release that introduces a governance contract carries a **Compatibility** section at the top of its notes, generated from the contract registry. `release-notes` provides a compatibility alias for viewing frozen notes. The version a cut is compared with comes from `version_source` in `.pose/policy/release.json` (`{\"path\": \"VERSION\", \"kind\": \"text\"}`, or `\"kind\": \"json\"` with a `key`): a project file holding `X.Y.Z`, with an optional `v` and `-dev` suffix. Only the engine repository may omit it; every other project must declare one. Manifest and tag integrity (spec `pose-release-manifest-tag-integrity`): a manifest is read through a strict contract (supported `schema_version`, no unknown fields, clean version and paths) that recomposes `specs`, `categories`, `breaking` and `release_input_digest` from the fragments it freezes, so an adulterated projection is refused instead of normalised. `check` and `status` verify a tag from its own tree: the tag (lightweight or annotated) is peeled to its commit, and the manifest, notes, frozen fragments and version source are read as blobs of that commit, never from the working tree. A tag whose tree holds no manifest is refused whatever the working tree holds; a later commit, provider evidence or the next cycle does not make a correct cut stale; a version source that cannot be recomputed from the manifest is reported as a limit.\n\n  **Query cursors:** paginated MCP list tools (`pose_list_specs`, `pose_list_roadmaps`, `pose_list_knowledge`, `pose_list_reports`, `pose_capability_history`) continue a query over the collection it was issued for. A cursor is signed, expires after 15 minutes and is bound to its tool, project, filters and the ordered collection with its index generation; a changed collection, another query, an expired token, a version 1 token or a server restart is refused with a `cursor-*` code and the caller restarts from the first page, never continued over a changed collection. A page is at most 500 items, discloses its `generation`, `collection` and `total`, and a missing `next_cursor` does not certify anything beyond that collection (spec `pose-generation-bound-query-cursors`).\n\n### MCP connection contract\n\nThe `pose context --task <xref> --json` command and the `pose_mcp_context` tool\nexpose the current authority and revision without revealing paths. Cross-project\nCLI writes require the target in `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` and the current\n`context_revision` through `--expect-context`; discovering a project through\n`HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` allows reading but does not grant write authorization.\nFor qualified creation, use `new-spec-qualified`: older engines refuse the\ncommand before interpreting new options as ignored arguments.\n\n- Treat `.mcp.json`, the connected server process and the selected project as\n  separate states.\n- Run `pose doctor --json` only for local static configuration; its\n  `mcp.config` finding reports `connection_checked: false`.\n- Call `pose_mcp_context` with an explicit `project_id` and optional qualified\n  `task_ref` before the first governed read and after changing workspaces,\n  configuration or binary version. Compare the returned server version,\n  connection instance and path-free `project_context.context_revision`.\n- Restart or reconnect the MCP client after changing `.mcp.json`; a running\n  stdio process does not reload client configuration automatically.\n- Enable `POSE_MCP_STRICT_PROJECT_SELECTION` for multi-project connections and\n  stop on `project_unknown` or `project_ambiguous` instead of falling back.\n- Use only authorized logical IDs returned by `pose_mcp_context`; the tool never\n  exposes filesystem roots.\n\n---\n\n## 7) CI policy\n\n- Run `pose check --strict` on every `pull_request` to `main`; treat failure as blocking.\n- Run `pose validate --strict` on every `pull_request` to `main`; treat `required` check failure as blocking.\n- Run the same workflow on `push` to `main` to detect post-merge drift.\n- Publish versionable artifacts per run: `pose-check.log`, `pose-validate.latest.log` and the `pose report` output.\n- Consume those artifacts during review instead of ephemeral job logs.\n\nA ready-made GitHub Action wrapping these gates ships with the POSE\ndistribution (`pose-action/`).\n\n### Interpreting failures\n\n- `POSE check (strict)` failure = structural break of the standard.\n- `POSE validate (strict, required gate)` failure = objective quality block.\n- `optional`-only failures = flagged technical risk; prioritize by criticality.\n\n### Phased rollout (recommended)\n\n1. Observability: PR workflow with artifacts, no new gates raised.\n2. Enforcement on `main`: strict `check` and `validate` as blocking gates; adjust `moduleOverrides` for modules not ready.\n3. Gradual expansion: promote mature checks from `optional` to `required` per domain.\n4. Hardening: review the matrix periodically, remove temporary exceptions.\n\n### Validation matrix per stack/module\n\n- Single source: [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json).\n- Base stacks: `node`, `go`, `rust`, `java` (Maven/Gradle).\n- `moduleOverrides` adjusts stack, mode and extra checks per module\n  (`pose init --wizard` seeds them from repository scan).\n- `required` in a `strict` or `tolerant` module → exit 1; `optional` failures don't block.\n- Logs use standardized `-> command` lines and a final summary, consumed by `pose report`.\n\n---\n\n## 8) `.pose/knowledge/` governance\n\nThe full loop (create → consult in workflows → schema validation → CI gate →\nhousekeeping) is available from installation; maturity comes from usage.\n\nWrite path: `pose new-knowledge <type> <slug>` generates an artifact from [`.pose/templates/knowledge.md`](.pose/templates/knowledge.md) with validated frontmatter.\n\nRead path: the [feature](.pose/workflows/feature.md), [bugfix](.pose/workflows/bugfix.md) and [review](.pose/workflows/review.md) workflows include \"consult `.pose/knowledge/`\" as a mandatory checklist step.\n\nGate: `pose knowledge-check --strict` validates the schema natively together with the overdue backlog; used in CI.\n\nCriteria for considering the subsystem continuously \"healthy\":\n\n- a dedicated knowledge-governance spec (create it with `pose new-spec` when activating the subsystem);\n- a dedicated rule, [`knowledge-governance.md`](.pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md);\n- defined ownership (e.g. `@pose-maintainers`) with biweekly/monthly review;\n- minimal housekeeping through `pose knowledge-housekeeping`.\n\nOn repeated neglect (overdue items untreated for 2 cycles), treat `knowledge`\nas degraded and block functional expansion until it recovers.\nThe transition from \"healthy\" to \"mature\" requires two consecutive cycles with\n`pose knowledge-check --strict` passing and at least one documented consultation\nper feature in active specs.\n\n---\n\n## 9) Instance limitations\n\n<!-- pose:instance-owned -->\n<!-- Keep the REAL limitations of your repository instance here, with evidence:\n     modules missing from module-metadata.json, stacks outside the matrix,\n     gates still tolerant and why. Note: this tracks instance setup, NOT engine bugs. -->\n\n- Document limitations as the instance evolves.\n\n---\n\n## 10) Instance next steps\n\n<!-- pose:instance-owned -->\n<!-- The operational backlog of POSE IN THIS repository (not product\n     features). Each item with an owner and a done criterion. -->\n\n1. Fill `.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json` for critical modules.\n2. Enable strict `check`/`validate` in CI (see §7).\n3. Run knowledge housekeeping on a recurring cycle.\n\n---\n\n## 11) POSE engine limitations & community feedback\n\n<!-- pose:instance-owned -->\n<!-- Keep real limitations of the POSE CLI / Engine itself here.\n     Report new engine limitations or proposals upstream via:\n     - `pose report-limitation --title \"...\" --kind limitation|bug|suggestion [--submit]`\n     - or directly on GitHub: https://github.com/oseiaspereira88/pose/issues -->\n\n- Document engine-level boundaries or bugs discovered during repository maintenance.\n- Community reports and feature proposals are automatically tracked at `.pose/feedback/` and upstream at `oseiaspereira88/pose`.\n\n---\n\n## 12) Executive summary\n\nPOSE is the operational layer that makes agent work reliable in the repository:\n\n- short instructions in [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md)\n- operational depth in [`.pose/`](.pose/)\n- assisted execution via [`pose`](pose) (CLI)\n- progressive maturity with skills in [`.agents/skills/`](.agents/skills/)\n\n## Open-Source POSE Contributor Mode\n\n<!-- pose:contributor-mode -->\n\nThis repository has enabled POSE Contributor Mode. Executing AI agents collaborate on improving POSE engine stability and developer experience under explicit user adjudication.\n\n- **Continuous Opportunity Scouting**: On every issue resolution or task execution, proactively scout for related improvements, edge cases, root-level parity gaps, or complementary corrections across adjacent tools and commands.\n- **User Consent & Explicit Adjudication**: Staging or submitting contributions upstream is ALWAYS an explicit user decision. Agents must ask the user for confirmation before staging drafts under `.pose/contributions/` or opening GitHub issues.\n- **Lifecycle & Status Integrity**: Track contribution states (`staged` -> `submitted` / `dismissed`) with `pose contribute list --status <staged|submitted|dismissed|all>`, `pose contribute submit <file>`, `pose contribute mark-submitted <file> [--issue-url <url>]`, or `pose contribute dismiss <file>`.\n- Check contributor status: `pose contribute status`\n- Stage a contribution manually: `pose contribute stage --type <bug|enhancement> --title <title>`\n- Disable contributor mode: `pose contribute disable`\n- Privacy guarantee: staged reports strictly isolate POSE engine mechanics and never leak proprietary code.\n## Opt-in progressive review profiles (ABM)\n\nAdd `engineering-judgment@1` and/or `high-criticality-review@1` explicitly to\n`overlay_profiles` in review policy schema v2 with component-aware review enabled.\nInstallation ships these profiles without activating them. Preview adoption with\n`pose review-plan spec:<slug> --json --explain` in an isolated copy first.\n\nBoth profiles match declared surface, capability, contract, infrastructure or\ngovernance delivery targets. The high-criticality profile additionally selects\n`high` and `critical` explicitly and raises independence to `different-actor`;\nan existing `mandatory-human` floor stays in force. They share three required\njudgments: `assumption-integrity`, `design-causality`, `solution-proportionality`.\nThe last includes negative space and speculative extensibility. Matching both\nprofiles yields one instance of each judgment and adds no tools.\n\nA target-free editorial scope receives no extra obligation, including in a high\ncomponent; a governance target with a Markdown entrypoint still matches. Missing\nmetadata remains diagnostic, not proof of low risk. See the authority contract\nbelow for verified identities.\n\n### Bands and the declared forecast\n\n`review-plan` reports a `band` (`baseline`, `elevated`, `critical`) and one\n`bands` entry per reason, each naming the `trigger` (the selector facts that\nmatched), the `basis` (`declared`, `observed`, `policy` or `unknown`), the\n`source` where the fact is readable, the `policy` ref that made it consequential\nand the `obligations` it produced. A criticality of `high`/`critical`, or a raise\nof reviewer independence, makes an entry `critical`; another matched overlay makes\nit `elevated`. An adopted overlay that could not decide a component — metadata\nmissing, or a selected field declared empty — yields a `band: unknown` entry with\nno obligations: uncertainty is shown, never charged, and never read as low risk.\n\n`projection` resolves the same plan over declared scope alone, labelled\n`declared-forecast`. When delivery provenance attributes more scope than the spec\ndeclared, `scope_expanded` is true and `observed_components`, `added_profiles`,\n`added_criteria`, `added_tools`, `raised_independence` and `raised_band` say which\nobligations only the observation produced. Each component also carries `origin`\n(`declared`, `observed` or `declared+observed`).\n\nBoth are derived from the fields the plan already resolved, through the same\nselectors and composer — there is no second policy engine — and neither enters\nthe plan digest. Publishing them therefore adds no obligation and supersedes no\nsealed review.\n\n### Observed structure and causal mapping\n\nDeclared metadata is the author's account of a change; it is a good first trigger\nand a poor last one. Adopt `structural-materiality@1` and a profile also selects\non what the subject was observed to do, read from both sides of the sealed\nsubject. `structural_kinds` composes conjunctively with the other selectors, and\nits vocabulary is closed to the kinds a detector can observe as material:\n`dependency`, `component`, `delivery-metadata`, `governance-contract`,\n`public-contract`, `submodule`.\n\nMateriality is bounded on purpose. A transitive dependency, a lock file, a rename\nand an unreadable manifest are all observed and all reported, and none is\nmaterial: the first two are consequences rather than decisions, a `component`\nwhose action is `changed` only restates the manifest edit beside it, and an\nunknown reading is uncertainty, which `structure.unknown` shows and never charges.\nThe validation matrix is read by check: a change that only appends checks under\nnames the matrix did not carry is reported as one `validation-check` fact per\ncheck and not charged, because registering a check strengthens what runs; any\nother change to the matrix, including an added check that reuses an existing\nname, stays a material `delivery-metadata` fact.\nEach path is compared over what the scope's own commits did to it, not over the\nsubject's `base..head`: a range spans every commit between the first and the last\nattributed one, so its diff also carries what other specs and releases changed in\nbetween. The sealed subject records, per path, the blobs at the ends of each\nuninterrupted run of attributed commits (`subject.attribution`), which keeps the\nreading content-addressed. A run that includes a commit whose `POSE-Spec:`\ntrailers also name other specs cannot be split by commit; its facts carry\n`shared_with`, and the plan warns that the reviewer should map the fact if this\nscope made the change, or answer its mapping `not-applicable` with the reason.\nA bundle sealed before this, or where Git cannot answer, is compared over its\nrange as before, and `range_observations` says whether that range is contaminated.\nNothing is resolved at all unless an adopted profile selects on structure, so a\nrepository that did not opt in pays nothing, not even the Git reads.\n\nUnlike the band summary, the material set enters the plan digest: a criterion that\nanswers for observed structure owes one answer per fact, so gaining a fact makes a\nsealed review stale instead of silently owing more.\n\nA criterion declaring `requires_structural_mapping` carries that obligation, and\nthe profile — not the engine — says which one. Under the `structural-causality`\ncontract, passing it requires one `mappings` entry per material fact: either a\n`basis` naming a requirement or constraint, or an assumption or decision that\nreaches one, or an explicit `missing-evidence`, `not-applicable` or\n`accepted-risk` with a rationale. Those three are kept apart because \"we have no\nevidence yet\", \"this does not apply\" and \"we accept this risk\" are three\nstatements a single blank used to hide. Record them with\n`pose review attest --mapping <criterion>|<delta>|<basis-or-disposition>|<why>`.\nAn approving attestation that verify would reject is refused before it is\nwritten, and the refusal lives in the domain writer every channel shares: the\nStore, `pose review attest` (preview, `--apply`, `--sign` and `--envelope`),\n`pose review auto-attest --apply` and the MCP `pose_review_attest` tool all get\nthe same typed refusal, with each reason the verifier gives, and the attempt\nwrites no attestation, bundle, supplement or event. The writer completes the\ncanonical identity first, judges the bundle's sealed gates plus the live\nsigning and trust requirements and the time of the new operation, and refuses\nthe approval of a bundle that a newer seal superseded or that no longer\nrepresents the current inputs. A trusted signature over invalid criteria still\nverifies its identity, but is never written as an approval; the MCP preview of\nan envelope returns `verify_blockers` and a refused apply returns\n`error_code: review_approval_refused`. A `changes-requested` or `rejected`\nattestation is an audit record: it is written without meeting the approval bar,\nand still needs the proof its channel requires. Records persisted before this\nare judged as they are, never rewritten.\n\nThe engine checks the namespace, the existence and the reach to a requirement —\npointing at a decision that reaches nothing states a choice without the\nrequirement that pays for it — and never checks whether the causal claim is true.\nThat is the reviewer's judgment. Disposing the criterion `not-applicable` while\nthe subject carries material facts is refused: an observed structural change is\nnot inapplicable, it is unmapped. Bundles sealed before the contract existed never\nlist it and keep their verdict.\n\nAdopting `causality_closeout_version: 1` in the review policy stamps a further\ncontract, `causality-closeout`, on new bundles and seals the band and the\ncontract-node digest of each spec in scope, so a material R/A/D change makes the\nreview stale. Under it a mapping to an invalidated or withdrawn assumption, or to\na withdrawn decision, is refused; a `mapped` answer needs its own rationale, and\none reason pasted across three or more facts on the same basis is refused as\ndisproportionate; `accepted-risk` needs an owner and a review date\n(`--mapping <criterion>|<delta>|accepted-risk|<why>|@owner|YYYY-MM-DD`) and never\nwaives a public or governance contract; `not-applicable` is refused while\nstructural coverage is unknown; and an elevated or critical band with material\nfacts but no criterion answering for them is refused, without requiring a human\nreviewer. Bundles sealed without the stamp keep their verdict.\n\nAdoption can be dated so it applies to the work that starts after it.\n`causality_closeout_adopted_at: YYYY-MM-DD` stamps the contract only on a scope\nwith a spec created on or after the date; a scope whose specs are all older is\nsealed without it and the bundle says why. `overlay_adopted_at` does the same per\noverlay — `{\"structural-materiality@1\": \"YYYY-MM-DD\"}` — so adopting an overlay\ndoes not charge its criteria, or a structural mapping, to work already under way;\na skipped overlay is named in the plan's explain trail and costs no Git read. A\nspec without a creation date is never exempted, and an undated overlay applies to\nevery scope it selects.\n\n### Protected policy baseline\n\nA diff that changes the review contract cannot be the authority that approves\nitself. When the reviewed scope touches `.pose/policy/` or `.pose/review-profiles/`,\nthe obligations are resolved a second time from the contract\nas it stood at the change set's resolved base, and whatever the diff weakened is\nrestored for that review: the stricter reviewer independence, a criterion the diff\ndropped (re-added with a `protected-baseline:` provenance), and a criterion the\ndiff softened from required to optional or from judged to collected. Restoration\nis one-directional, so a baseline weaker than the diff changes nothing. Those\ntwo directories are the contract — policy and profiles carry their own schema\nversions — while rule bodies stay outside the gate, because it is the criterion\ncontract that is compared.\n\n`policy_baseline` reports `protected`, the `revision`, the contract `paths`, the\n`weakened` contract changes observed — including `allow_approved_with_reservations`,\n`require_signed_attestations`, `identity_assurance` and `component_aware` — and the\n`restored` obligations. A contract change whose base revision cannot be resolved is\nreported unprotected, with a warning and an `unknown` band: POSE states that the\nreview is unprotected rather than implying it was protected.\n\nTwo cases used to make a contract change unreviewable, and no longer do: a diff\nthat sets `enabled: false`, and a diff that removes or breaks the profile the\npolicy points at. Both now resolve the plan from the protected contract and record\nthe weakening. Adopting a weaker contract stays possible; approving it under the\nweaker contract does not. Restorations reach the plan digest through the criteria,\nthe independence and the explain trail, so a protected plan has its own identity.\n\n## Verified review authority (ABM)\n\nWhen a review policy sets `identity_assurance` to `verified` for a scope kind,\n`agent:`/`human:` prefixes remain labels only. The attestation must carry a\n`ReviewAuthorityClaim` signed by a trusted Ed25519 envelope, bound to the exact\nbundle digest, project audience, reviewer principal and executions required by\nthe review plan. A human role also needs a dedicated authority grant. Existing\nbundles without this sealed gate remain auditable in `declared` mode; POSE does\nnot infer cognitive independence or require Harne8 for offline review.\n\nA claim carries two bindings, both read from the protected review policy and\nnever from the environment. `project` must equal `authority_project`, the project\nid the decision governs; `audience` must equal `authority_audience`, the verifier\ninstallation the claim is addressed to. One installation can serve several\nprojects, so the audience alone would let a claim issued for one of them satisfy\nanother; verified assurance therefore requires both keys, and each mismatch is\nrefused with its own reason. Action-request claims under `verified` follow the\nsame rule.\n\nEvery review surface discloses what its record proves. `review-plan` prints the\nidentity assurance in force; `review verify`, `review-check` and\n`closeout-check` print one assurance line and carry an `assurance` object in\nJSON with three separation readings kept apart: `separation_required` (the\nplan), `separation_declared` (what the record's identity strings say) and\n`separation_verified` (what a signed claim bound to the bundle established, or\n`not-verified`). Under `declared`, `human:` and `agent:independent-` are shown\nas declarations. `cognitive_independence` is always `not-observable`.\n\nAttribution is recorded apart from identity. `pose review attest` accepts\n`--prepared-by`, `--concluded-by`, `--confirmed-by` with\n`--confirmation-mode adopted-conclusions|authorized-operation`, and\n`--applied-by`; none of them is ever filled from `--reviewer`, and a\nconfirmation is bound to the digest of the exact decision, criteria, tools and\nfindings written. Adopting conclusions and authorizing an operation render\ndifferently, and a confirmation is `verified` only when a verified authority\nclaim names that human. Records written before this carry no block and render\nas `legacy-undifferentiated`. `pose review attribution-supplement` clarifies an\nexisting record by reference, append-only, and cannot add a confirmation.\n\n### Historical review claims and criterion reuse\n\nReview assurance discloses `temporal`: signed issuer-asserted `attested_at`,\nclaim validity at that historical time, current claim validity and current pin\ntrust. These fields never establish observed historical authorization. Expiry\nis inclusive at equality, no TTL is imposed, and second-resolution historical\ntimestamps retain their bounded precision. New operations use their own time;\nexpired approving writes are refused before artifact creation. Current pins and\nhuman grants remain the revocation boundary, including after key rotation.\n\n`reused_from[].mode` can be `verified-source` or `current-reaffirmation`. The\nformer validates source proof, bundle bindings, sealed criteria, decision,\nfindings and recursive provenance. The latter records a current passed judgment\nwith a conclusion and keeps its source reference. An absent mode preserves\nlegacy behavior and is disclosed as `legacy-reaffirmation`, without a verified\nhistorical-pass claim. Verify, review-check, closeout, CLI and MCP consume the\nsame domain result. Source traversal is operation-local: 16 levels, 32 distinct\nattestations, 128 edges and 16 MiB of source files. A cycle, unreadable source or\nexceeded budget is inconclusive and cannot satisfy verified-source.\n\nThis additive contract is classified for a minor release. It adopts no policy,\nrewrites no historical record and creates no receipt retrospectively.\n\n### Action lifecycle authority\n\nAn answer proof authorizes its answer only. New waiver, cancellation and supersession events carry `lifecycle_version: 1`. Under verified action policy they require an operation-bound SSHSIG (`pose-action-lifecycle`) or a trusted issuer's schema-2 action claim. MCP always requires this proof. Both supplied emitters must verify; human presence follows the current explicit channel scope.\n\nUse `pose action waive` for a recipient/role authority's dispensation, and `pose action cancel` only to withdraw the requester's own question. Cancellation does not waive an independent producer's condition. With declared local policy, these operations remain declared and are disclosed as such. Unsigned legacy lifecycle events remain labeled legacy; they cannot dispense a condition under verified policy. Reads reverify signatures and issuer bindings at the recorded historical acceptance time, using current issuer trust pins. They do not prove historical role membership or hardware provenance.\n\nPreview a lifecycle operation with the usual request digest, expected revision, idempotency key and reason. The JSON preview returns the exact statement. Sign it through `--sign` / `--signature`, or pass a schema-2 `--claim` file. Offline statements use `pose action statement <act-id> --operation waived|cancelled|superseded --actor <principal> --expected-revision <n> --idempotency-key <key> --reason <text>`. Supersession statements also need `--successor-id` and `--successor-digest` from the replacement preview. The signed claim binds `operation`, `expected_revision`, `idempotency_key`, `reason`, `successor_id` and `successor_digest`, in addition to the existing request/project/audience/principal/time fields; schema-1 answer claims cannot authorize retirement.\n\nPreserve the preview's `requested_at` using `--requested-at` (CLI) or `requested_at` (MCP); it is validated as RFC3339 and keeps successor identity stable while signing. This timestamp is request metadata, not authenticated wall-clock provenance. Opening a replacement with `--supersedes` requires the predecessor's requester or recipient authority. With verified policy, pass `--authority <project-relative JSON>` containing actor, request_digest, expected_revision, idempotency_key, reason and signature or claim/envelope, plus the exact successor_id/successor_digest returned by the open preview. MCP `pose_action_open` accepts the same `authority` object, always with proof. Creation alone never grants authority to retire the predecessor. Authorization is checked before creation and again under the predecessor journal lock. An authorized replacement can be opened but not retire the predecessor if an intervening revision/policy change prevents the append; the error names the partial opening. Cross-journal recovery belongs to B10.\n\nMCP `pose_action_resolve` defaults to `operation: answered`; `cancelled` and `waived` require a reason and return a lifecycle statement in preview. Arbitrary actor `invalidate` calls are refused. Subject-change invalidation is derived by the engine from the original and current material digests; transfer invalidation remains a governed engine path. These observations do not require a new human signature and never grant waiver authority. No policy, issuer or consumer pin is adopted automatically. The stricter authorization semantics require release compatibility review before publication.\n\n### Action recovery and preflight\n\nPreview `pose action resolve`, `cancel`, `waive` and replacement `open` through the same readonly domain admission as apply. Invalid actors, roles, options, request digests, revisions, projects and states are refused. Missing identity/presence proof is disclosed as `proof_pending: true` with `acceptance: not-certified`; no preview invokes a signer or certifies future acceptance. Apply repeats current authority, proof, state and revision checks under the journal lock. `pose action statement` checks answer admissibility before emitting bytes for an old material subject. An identical semantic idempotency key/payload records no second decision; a conflicting payload is refused. A renewed valid proof may acknowledge the same semantic decision without replacing its historical event.\n\n`next_step` in request views, presentation, obligations, readiness restrictions and closeout diagnostics, and `next_steps` in Attention, distinguishes `answer`, `replace-request`, `repair-subject`, `remediate-or-waive`, `resume-supersession` and `repair`. These are instructions and requirements, never proof of authority or automatic effects. An invalidated request requires a materially updated replacement via `pose action open --supersedes`; missing material needs repair. Decline remains pending: remediate or obtain an authorized waiver with `pose action waive` / MCP `pose_action_resolve` (`operation: waived`). Repeating identical material with a new timestamp or lineage is refused. Material digests detect declared content changes; they do not judge whether rewritten text constitutes adequate semantic remediation. A satisfied answer is not asked again. Partial recovery offers the same prepared `open`, never an answer against an old digest.\n\nSupersession reserves one immutable intent in `.pose/actions/supersessions/<predecessor>.json`. Preserve `requested_at`, predecessor revision/digest, successor ID/digest, retirement idempotency key, actor and reason for retry. `pose action open` / MCP `pose_action_open` resumes the same intent, checking current authority/proof before the remaining retirement. A competing intent is refused. The predecessor journal lock is nonblocking and held through reservation, successor creation and retirement; the writer takes no second journal lock and makes one attempt per call. Recovery phases are `reserved`, `successor-opened`, `complete` or `repair-required`; incomplete incoming/outgoing links stay restrictive. A successor cannot become a new predecessor before its incoming retirement completes. Both records remain visible until convergence. Completed retries acknowledge recorded history rather than applying another effect.\n\nIntent files are regular, project-local, schema-checked and bounded to 64 KiB. A partial intent or journal requires explicit inspection/repair, including the missing identity/revision/material data; no command silently truncates, discards a reservation or retries forever. Lock contention and append/Sync errors retain the phase and original journals. Command refusal has `audit_persisted: false`: it is a diagnostic, not a journal `refused` event, and changes no legitimate question revision or idempotency history. Existing historical events remain readable. Upgrade all writers sharing the journals before adoption; older writers cannot enforce the intent reservation. This breaking behavior needs compatibility review before release; no policy, issuer or consumer pin is adopted.\n\n### Amendment history before close\n\nThe first non-baseline `pose amend` on an empty history copies the valid completed atomic start baseline before recording the requested change. It uses the recorded hashes/state/relations, not edited text; the new baseline event records the current declared author/time, not past approval. Schema 1 keeps R nodes only; schema 2 requires `contract_nodes_version: 1`. An invalid or incomplete start refuses the append. No start means legacy behavior; an existing journal is never silently repaired. Explicit `--baseline` continues to snapshot the current state.\n\nAmend and close share the per-spec lifecycle lock. The append writes baseline then change and calls Sync; failures are reported without truncating or rewriting history. A partial or malformed journal requires explicit inspection/repair. `lint-spec` warns about pending amendment findings on `in-progress` specs and rejects them on `done`; `pose close` checks the same history before transition and under lock. No history remains opt-out; no policy is adopted automatically.\n\n### Terminal closeout verification\n\nLifecycle `done` records the transition; terminal verification reads the applicable review contract and current opted-in amendment/action/federation gates. `pose closeout-check`, `pose close --plan` and MCP `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` share that verifier. A done plan exposes `lifecycle_done`, `verification` with `verification_basis` and `limitations`, and `next_action`; historical pipeline steps are `not-run`, not inferred as executed. `verify` reports the current query's verdict. `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` also returns a typed `handoff` when the plan has steps left and nothing blocks it: the `pose close --apply --digest <full plan digest>` invocation as structured arguments, the arguments the performer still owes (`required_inputs`, the `--reviewer` identity when a mechanical attestation is pending), the project the caller selected (by its logical id and the digest of its root, never a path), the digests it was computed from, the authority it needs and when it stops being valid; it executes nothing and is not an approval (no handoff is returned for a plan that is terminal, blocked or on an unknown read view). Every MCP tool has a declared route class (domain-direct, cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff), and `pose_suggest` and `pose_get_followups` run the CLI command in process unless `POSE_EXECUTABLE` is set or the installed binary was replaced since the server started, in which case they are spawned. Existing review cutoffs and sealed historical contracts remain applicable; a legacy exemption is disclosed without inventing an execution receipt.\n\nFor a done scope, `pose close --apply|--resume` is readonly: verified state returns `already-satisfied` with empty `steps_run` and `effects`, while missing required proof or invalid history returns blocked with a supported repair action. Neither outcome rewrites checkpoints, starts, amendments or lifecycle. Plain `pose close` also verifies instead of repeating the transition. An in-progress plan checks the existing amendment gate before sealing. No policy, review or historical receipt is created automatically.\n\n### Adopt delegated review\n\nRun `pose init` in a new project to receive an interactive delegation offer,\nwith yes as the default. Accepting it detects Codex and Claude CLIs on PATH\nwithout executing them, creates their read-only profiles, and provisions a\nproject-specific native executor issuer outside the repository. Its pin grants\nattestation signing only, never human authority. The decision is enabled last.\nNo supported CLI, a refusal, EOF or non-interactive init leaves an explicit\n`delegated_review: false`, without an agent policy or signing key. Existing\ninstances are preserved; `pose update` only suggests adoption.\n\nFor an existing project, configure `.pose/policy/delegation.json` with a\nreview-capable agent, an explicit executor, `provenance.trust` set to\n`executor-attested` and `conclusions.issuer`. Pin that exact local issuer for\nattestations and bind `authority_project` to the declared project and a nonempty\n`authority_audience`. Inspect `pose adopt delegated-review`, then apply with\n`--apply`. The command validates the signer and records the adoption date.\n`pose adopt delegated-review --off --apply` records an explicit disabled\ndecision and date while preserving issuers, pins and execution history.\n`--decline` and `--defer` record configuration decisions; they do not implicitly\nchange the review policy.\n\nThe B17 signed-run-proof barrier stays live: adoption can invalidate an old\nprefix-only approval under current verification without rewriting its record.\n\nAn adopted capability requires different-actor review: a verified run from\nanother vendor or model. When automatic routing cannot obtain that separation,\nthe engine opens a maintainer action request instead of weakening the plan.\nA mandatory-human plan also escalates. Only an explicit disabled decision\nallows new different-actor plans to use same-actor-separate-execution, disclosed\nas `delegated-review-disabled` in the sealed plan, attestation, verification and\ncloseout. Legacy omission retains its previous behavior; mandatory-human and a\nstronger protected baseline remain stronger.\n\nFor an agent review of the change that disables an adopted baseline, the\nsealed plan retains `delegated_run_proof_required`. Independent routing and\nautomatic verified conclusion remain available for that protected review only;\nunrelated off-capability work stays off. A reviewer prefix alone cannot approve\nthe disabling change.\n\nIf init fails after saving the agent policy or key, preserve those artifacts,\ninspect the reported publication state, finish pinning that issuer and retry\n`pose adopt delegated-review --apply`. A durability-unconfirmed publication\nrequires inspection before retrying. An enabled decision never proves a vendor\nreceipt, human identity or cognitive independence. Use the\n[pose-delegate skill](.agents/skills/pose-delegate/SKILL.md) to select review\nversus task and follow native next actions.\n\n### Delegated executions (`pose delegate`)\n\n`pose review` governs the subject (bundle, brief, attestation, verify); `pose delegate` governs executions. An **agent** is a CLI profile configured in `.pose/policy/delegation.json`; a **run** is one execution; an **attempt** is a run and its continuations; a **thread** is the rounds of one scope. `pose delegate review`, `adjudicate` and `smoke` run the sealed bundle's generated brief on a disposable worktree at the sealed commit with read access only, under the attempt ledger and containment below; any other `--access` is refused. The agent never records an attestation: the engine records the conclusion of a completed review run only as the conclusions of delegated reviews below describe. Without `--agent` (or with `auto`) routing chooses among the agents of the purpose: first by `routing.prefer` (default `opposite-vendor-of-implementation`, in the order the policy declares its agents), then by the steps of `routing.on_unavailable` (default `same-vendor-other-model`, `wait`, `same-model`). `wait` applies only when every earlier candidate is limited with a known reset within `routing.max_wait` (default 5h) and starts the run after the first reset, without polling. The implementation routed against is the identity the bundle sealed; for a bundle sealed before bundles recorded it, the policy's `provenance.implementation`, else `--lead`. An agent named with `--agent` bypasses routing and warns when it shares the implementation's vendor. Every run records the decision (`routing`: the candidates, why each was skipped, the step taken) and `independence_achieved` (`different-vendor`, `different-model` or `same-model`); with no agent available the run does not start and the error carries the decision. `--label` names the run, `--lead <vendor>[/<model>]` declares the implementation when the bundle records none, `--dry-run` prints the resolved agent, purpose, access, working directory and argv (authorized credential values redacted) and reserves nothing, and `--json` prints one versioned document (`pose.delegate.run/v1`, `plan/v1`, `ls/v1`, `cancel/v1`, `agents/v1`, `error/v1`), usage errors included. On `ls`, `resume`, `cancel` and `agents` the same flags select: a run or an agent that does not match is not listed, or is refused when named; `--dry-run` changes nothing anywhere. Messages follow `POSE_LOCALE` (`en` or `pt-BR`). A run reference is a run id, an attempt id, a label or a session prefix of six characters or more; an ambiguous reference exits 2 and lists the candidates.\n\nA schema-2 agent may opt in to `review_profile.command` and `review_profile.resume_command` for governed review only. Omission preserves its existing read adapter; tasks, adjudication and smoke keep their commands. A resumable agent must declare the profile's resumed command with `{session}`. Once selected, failure never falls back to the read or task profile. The selected commands remain bound by policy/provenance verification. Older strict parsers reject the new field. The configured Claude event codec canonicalizes repeated typed init records only for the same parsed session identity before session recognition. Different identities and generic regex ambiguity are still refused; raw provenance/redaction and limit/reset channels retain the original bytes.\n\nThe sandboxed Claude review profile that pose-dist qualified for its own reviews uses safe/restricted mode, Read/Grep/Glob/Bash only, mandatory shell sandboxing (`failIfUnavailable:true`, `allowUnsandboxedCommands:false`), and a read-only sealed source tree. Both fresh and resumed wrappers require bubblewrap, socat and jq on Linux (jq remains required on macOS), with installation advice on refusal. Go cache/temp writes go to private operation-owned scratch outside the checkout; the existing module cache is read, and unavailable downloads/tests that write into source can fail. The profile instructs the reviewer to run `go clean -cache` as its final Bash command inside the sandbox; the engine independently retains incomplete cleanup as a failed run under its unchanged limits. Never clean an adapter-controlled cache outside the sandbox: a test can replace its path with a symlink. This qualifies Claude's shell subprocess boundary, not isolation of the trusted Claude executable or arbitrary credential reads. Checkout integrity checks still apply. Installing engine code does not adopt the profile in another instance; copy and qualify its explicit policy block before use. The wrapper also denies the resolved physical Git common directory, because Claude auto-allows it for worktrees. For this opt-in profile, POSE prepares only empty `.claude/.cc-writes` directories at the disposable checkout root and declared components before taking its integrity snapshot; added files and all later mutations still fail. Final sandboxed cleanup includes leftover `go-build*` directories directly under GOTMPDIR after all jobs finish.\n\n`pose delegate ls` lists attempts by their latest run with state, purpose, agent, label, turns, age and last step — for an interrupted run also its cause, whether a partial draft exists, the reset instant and the time left — and the runs in progress with the liveness of their process. `pose delegate cancel <run>` stops a run in progress; it is recorded `failed` with the cause `cancelled`, and its attempt stays consumed. `pose delegate agents` lists each agent with vendor, model, whether its CLI is on the path, the reset instant of its last recorded usage limit and the state routing reads: `available`, `limited until <instant>` (its last run hit a usage limit whose reset is ahead), `missing` (its CLI is not on the path), `unknown` (the run history could not be read whole) or `probe-failed` (its optional `probe` failed within its bound). Availability is read from the path and the recorded runs; only a configured probe runs anything. `pose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke --detach` starts the run as a process of its own, in a session of its own that survives the caller's terminal, and returns at once with the attempt (a run reference) and the path of its log; a run whose route must first wait for a usage-limit reset is not detached. `pose delegate wait <run> [--timeout <d>] [--json]` is a plain foreground process that ends when the run does and prints one line — run, state, decision, resumable flag, reset instant — with the exit code `0` approved, `10` changes requested or rejected, `20` failed (a completed run with no decision too), `30` interrupted, `40` stalled, `50` the wait timed out, and `2` for a usage error; every waiter is released, a run already ended returns at once, and killing a wait leaves the run running. The process running an attempt rewrites a heartbeat in its marker every 10 seconds; a run whose heartbeat is older than three intervals and whose process is provably gone is recorded `stalled` by the first reader, through an exclusive claim every reader then agrees on, and `ls` shows each running run's heartbeat age. `pose delegate watch [<run>|<label>]` shows an attempt as one task: the runs it recorded are replayed with a marker where a run resumed another and the interrupted run naming the run that continued it, then a running attempt is followed through the normalized, redacted events its process writes as they come, until the record is published; `--no-follow` prints what exists and exits, `-v` adds the tail of each tool's output (`--lines <n>`, default 12) and `--json` prints NDJSON. With no reference it follows the only running run; at a terminal it otherwise opens fzf with a preview of the run under the cursor refreshed every `--refresh <d>` (default 2s, 0 turns it off), or a numbered menu without fzf; outside a terminal an absent reference exits 2 and lists the candidates. Colours appear only on a terminal and follow `NO_COLOR`. A live event is redacted with the credentials and the session references the stream has declared so far; the events bound to the record are derived again from the whole transcript. `on_terminal` on an agent (or else on a purpose) is a command run once after a run ends, with only `POSE_DELEGATE_RUN` and `POSE_DELEGATE_STATE` in its environment, the run directory as working directory, its own process group and a bound (`timeout`, default 30s, at most 5m); its failure is recorded beside the run, shown by `ls`, and never changes the run's state. `pose delegate handoff <run> --to <agent|auto>` continues a run that stopped interrupted or failed, short of a conclusion, with another agent under the same attempt and its continuation budget, which is the `resume.max_resumes` and `max_wall_time` of the agent that opened the attempt (3 and 60 minutes by default) whichever agent continues it, so changing agents never widens it; a completed run, the same agent and a run already continued are refused, and a run handed off is no longer resumed. Its brief is the predecessor's generated brief followed by a labelled section: the predecessor's partial final message and the commands it ran with their exit status, paired by the call identifier the agent's stream records and otherwise left unknown rather than guessed, each with its digest, quoted so no line of it reads as a decision, bounded at 16 KiB with the cut disclosed, and an instruction to re-verify. Only the handoff run concludes. The record binds the base brief digest and the section digest in `handoff_digest`, names its predecessor in `hands_off_from`, and a signed proof verifies the section against the predecessor's record. `--to auto` routes as the purpose does, the stopped agent excluded and unavailable ones skipped. A foreground run or handoff that stops interrupted in a terminal offers waiting for its reset and resuming, handing off, or cancelling, which is offered even alone, and does nothing until answered; the stopping command's result, `ls --json` and `wait --json` carry the same ways forward as `next_actions`, each with its command, whether it is available now — judged by the planning its command runs, budget and wait bound included — and why not, and a `cancel` that leaves the run stopped. `watch` marks a handoff with `⇄` beside the `↻` of a resume. `pose delegate task <prompt-file|->` hands any task to another agent: the prompt goes on stdin in `--cd` (default: the current directory) with the agent's command for `--access read|sandboxed|write|full`; the default is `purposes.task.default_access` (read when unset), `full` is never a default and prints a warning naming what it disables. A task's template places `{add_dirs}`, `{no_network}` and `{outside_git}`, filled from the agent's `add_dir_args` (with `{dir}`), `no_network_args` and `outside_git_args`; `--add-dir`, `--no-network` and `--effort` that the agent's template cannot carry are refused with exit 2, and `--no-network` with `write` or `full` too. The final message goes to stdout and the agent's exit code is returned; `--json` replaces both with one document. A task runs in the real directory: containment bounds its time and output, not its filesystem. Its record (prompt, stream, error stream, normalized events, argv, removed variable names, exit code, label) is owner-only under the user's state directory (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/pose/delegate/`, where `XDG_STATE_HOME` counts only as an absolute path and `~/.local/state` is the default), or, with `--record`, in the `delegate-runs` directory of `.pose`. The state directory may be reached through a link of the user's own; resolved, it and every directory above it must belong to the user or to root and be writable by no group or other user unless sticky, and `pose`, `pose/delegate` and the project's directory below it must be real directories of the user's that no group or other user can write; a task refuses to record or read anywhere else and names the directory (on Windows only links are refused). `--detach` starts a task as the review purposes start a detached run and returns its attempt, its log and the place of its record; a prompt piped to `task -` or `resume --prompt -` is read before detaching and written to the detached run through a pipe, never a file, and a refusal before the attempt is reserved returns the foreground's document and exit code. A detached call keeps its log and hand-back in `.pose/review-private/supervisors`, which, with every directory up to the project root, must be the user's and writable by no group or other user, and whose root's ancestors must belong to the user or root and be writable by no one else unless sticky; the root is resolved once and only that resolved root is used, a caller whose current directory is gone is refused, and a project's private task history is keyed by its resolved root (records an earlier engine kept under a linked spelling are still read). A detached call believes a hand-back only once a piped prompt is through and a run reserved the attempt it names. A checkout that fails this can still run in the foreground; on Windows only links and non-directories are refused, and ACLs are not checked. A task is never evidence: no review reader reads it, `pose review attest --run` refuses it by name, and `wait` returns 0 when the agent succeeded, with no decision. `ls`, `watch`, `wait`, `cancel`, `resume` and `handoff` work on tasks; `resume` without `--prompt` sends a default continuation message, with the agent's `resume.commands` for the task's access, and `--agent auto` routes against the launching session. A task can also continue a session POSE never recorded, such as one a personal launcher opened, with `--session <id> --agent <name>`: the agent's resume command for the access runs with that session, the prompt optional, and the record says it continued a session without keeping it. `pose review dispatch`, `resume` and `status`, the aliases this surface deprecated, were removed: each exits 2 and names its `pose delegate` replacement.\n\n`delegation.json` is schema 2: `agents` (vendor, model, `cli`, `events`, per-access `commands`, `effort`, `timeout`, `limit`, `session`, `resume`, `executor`, `containment`, `on_terminal`, `probe` — `command` and an optional `timeout` up to 60s, default 10s, with no placeholder), `routing` (`prefer`, `on_unavailable`, `lead_models` as `{vendor: model}` in lower case, `max_wait` up to 24h), per-purpose budgets (`purposes.<review|adjudicate|smoke|task>.max_runs_per_bundle`, `timeout`) and `env_unset`. Command templates are argv arrays; `{output}`, `{session}` and `{effort}` are whole elements, never interpolated, and `{effort}` becomes the argv fragment of the agent's effort level. An agent's own `max_runs_per_bundle` wins over the purpose's; a purpose's `timeout` wins over the agent's. `pose update` converts `reviewers.json` (schema 1) into `delegation.json` deterministically and removes it: each adapter becomes an agent whose `read` command is the adapter's command, a budget all adapters share moves to the review-path purposes, and every agent projects exactly the adapter it came from. The converted file is archived outside `.pose/policy` and named in `migrated_from`, so a run recorded before the conversion keeps its policy baseline while `delegation.json` is exactly that conversion; editing `delegation.json` ends that, as any policy change does. The engine reads `reviewers.json` while `delegation.json` is absent and refuses when both exist. A schema 1 adapter that interpolates `{output}` or `{session}` inside an argument (in `sh -c` code, for example) is not converted: `pose update` warns and keeps `reviewers.json` until the template passes the placeholder as a whole element.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_version\": 2,\n  \"agents\": {\n    \"codex\": {\n      \"vendor\": \"openai\", \"model\": \"gpt-6.1-sol\", \"cli\": \"codex\", \"events\": \"codex-jsonl\", \"timeout\": \"60m\",\n      \"commands\": {\"read\": [\"codex\", \"exec\", \"-m\", \"gpt-6.1-sol\", \"{effort}\", \"-s\", \"read-only\", \"--json\", \"-o\", \"{output}\", \"-\"]},\n      \"effort\": {\"default\": \"medium\", \"levels\": {\"medium\": [\"-c\", \"model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\"\"], \"high\": [\"-c\", \"model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\"\"]}}\n    },\n    \"claude\": {\n      \"vendor\": \"anthropic\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-5-5\", \"cli\": \"claude\", \"events\": \"claude-stream-json\", \"timeout\": \"60m\",\n      \"commands\": {\"read\": [\"claude\", \"-p\", \"--model\", \"claude-opus-5-5\", \"--permission-mode\", \"plan\", \"--output-format\", \"stream-json\", \"--verbose\"]}\n    }\n  },\n  \"purposes\": {\"review\": {\"max_runs_per_bundle\": 3}}\n}\n```\n\nThe child process receives none of the parent agent's session variables (`CLAUDECODE`, `CLAUDE_CODE_*` session markers, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, `CODEX_SESSION_ID` and the other names Claude Code and Codex set for their children), nor any name in `env_unset`, even when the containment allowlist names them; the run records the removed names in `env_removed`, never their values. An agent's `events` (`codex-jsonl`, `claude-stream-json` or `plain`) selects the parser that turns its redacted stream into normalized events — turn, message, tool call, tool output, usage limit, final message, error — kept beside the run's transcript as `<run>.events.ndjson` and bound to the record by `events_digest`. An agent that writes no `{output}` file has its draft taken from the final message its stream carries. A run in progress is known by a private marker under `.pose/review-private/active/`, which names its process the way a start lock does.\n\n#### Retiring a personal launcher\n\nA personal launcher — a script, skill or note of one person that runs another vendor's agent and follows its logs — retires once the parity check passes: `go test ./internal/cli -run 'LauncherParity|ActiveDocument' -count=1` in `pose-mcp`, registered as the `launcher-parity` check of the validation matrix. The distribution's parity matrix (`delegation-launchers.json`, in the POSE repository, not in an installed instance) maps every capability the delegation ADR lists for the launchers to the `pose delegate` command that replaces it and the tests that cover it; the check fails while a row has no command or test, names a test or a flag that does not exist, or the ADR lists a capability no row carries, and a second check fails while an active document, skill or help text names a replaced command or launcher outside a removal note. POSE never changes files outside the repository, so the operator retires the launcher:\n\n1. Declare the agents in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` from the launcher's own invocations: a command per access, `resume`, `limit`, `session`, `events`, `outside_git_args`, `add_dir_args` and `no_network_args`; `pose delegate agents` shows what was read.\n2. Replace each use: a review with `pose delegate review`, an adjudication with `pose delegate adjudicate`, any other task with `pose delegate task`, a continuation of a run POSE recorded with `pose delegate resume` or `pose delegate handoff` (they reuse its agent, access and settings), a session the launcher left interrupted with `pose delegate task --session <id> --agent <name>` — passing the settings the launcher used (`--access`, `--cd`, `--add-dir`, `--effort`, `--no-network`), which POSE cannot recover from a session it never recorded; the run it records then resumes with `pose delegate resume`, reusing them — a background run with `--detach` and `pose delegate wait`, following with `pose delegate watch` and listing with `pose delegate ls`.\n3. Remove the launcher from the `PATH`, and the skills, aliases and notes that tell a person or an agent to call it.\n4. Keep its old run logs only as read-only history; POSE does not import them, and a review they hold stays what it was.\n\nHistorical records — ADRs, reports, roadmaps, changelogs, release manifests, done specs and knowledge — keep naming the launchers and the removed aliases as written.\n\n### Delegated review attempts and history\n\n`pose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke <scope|bundle>` reserves an immutable attempt before launching the configured agent. A completed run on the unchanged bundle requires an explicit `--reason <text>` for every further dispatch, including another agent or brief kind. Quota is re-read at execution and includes failed and reserved attempts; a preview is no future authorization. Concurrent calls compete for one ordinal slot and a loser refuses without launching or retrying. Reasons are one line, at most 2048 bytes; keep secrets and session identifiers out of them.\n\nReservations live in `.pose/review-attempts/`, final receipts link the run, and `.pose/review-run-ledger/` witnesses exact run bytes and transcript digests. Every publication uses the exclusive synced publisher. New runs carry `ledger_version: 1`, `attempt_id` and the optional reason; legacy schema-1 runs retain their original identities, with no invented reservation or signature. An incomplete reservation, missing witness/run/transcript, inconsistent receipt, unknown record or exhausted read budget is explicitly incomplete. Inspect it; no automatic deletion, truncation, repair or retry occurs. Upgrade writers sharing these directories together. Run continuity and authenticated provenance remain separate deliveries.\n\n`pose history-check --strict` detects missing or rewritten witnessed runs and Git modifications/deletions of tracked immutable run, ledger and attempt files, including staged or committed changes. History containing only delegated records is supported. Git/filesystem trust is required: erasing unanchored evidence and its witnesses cannot be proved from that same erased storage. No cryptographic append-only storage or vendor identity is claimed. Records are limited to 1 MiB, inventories to 10000 entries, run JSON reads to 32 MiB per operation, transcripts to 256 MiB each and 512 MiB per verification, and Git history observation to 8 MiB/30 seconds; exceeding a bound is unknown, never a clean truncated history.\n\n`pose review verify` and MCP disclose scope-wide prior runs alongside the attestation, including earlier rejecting drafts. `pose insights` aliases `pose stats`; text, JSON, HTML and MCP `pose_insights` show runs per bundle, attempts, reruns and per-adapter declared draft approval rates. A time window limits counted events, while the full preceding history classifies reruns. Approval rate uses unambiguous completed, unsuperseded `decision: approved|changes-requested|rejected` drafts; ambiguous output, failures and stale runs are unknown. Legacy unreserved runs count as individual attempts without claiming a reservation existed. Draft labels and rates are not verified approvals and never apply an attestation or enable delegated-review automatically.\n\n**Optional worktree runtime.** `pose runtime start` starts, detached, a runtime for this worktree; `attach` and `status` handshake with it, `stop` drains it (`--drain <d>`, default 5s) and says how many requests in flight finished and how many were abandoned, `restart` is stop then start, and `serve` runs it in the foreground (`--idle <d>`, default 30m, stops an idle runtime; 0 never does). It is opt-in: no other command starts it, and every command runs standalone whether it runs or not. The runtime is identified by the user, the canonical worktree (symlinks resolved) and the Git common directory, so two worktrees of one repository, or two users, never share one; the handshake compares the engine, build, protocol and schema as well and an attach from another engine is refused naming the field. Its endpoint is a Unix domain socket (Linux, macOS) of mode 0600 in a 0700 directory owned by the user (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pose`, else `pose-runtime-<uid>` under the temporary directory, or `POSE_RUNTIME_DIR`), whose peer the operating system identifies before a byte is read; a link, a loose mode, another owner or a file that is not a socket is refused, and a socket nobody answers on is replaced. There is no TCP and no token. Slice 1 hosts no domain operation: it answers handshake, status and stop, confers no authority, signature or presence, and every restart has a new epoch, inheriting nothing. On Windows, which has no named-pipe implementation here, `status` reports `unsupported` and every command runs standalone. `status` exits 0 for running, standalone and unsupported and 1 for an incompatible runtime; `attach` also exits 1 when there is nothing to attach to. `--json` prints the document `pose.runtime.status/1`.\n\n**Hosted checks and the resident Git memo.** `pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <command> [args...]` runs `review-check`, `closeout-check`, `artifact-check`, `surface-check` or `roadmap-check` in the runtime of the worktree when one is running, compatible and willing, and standalone in this process otherwise; the exit code and the output are the command's, and `--envelope` prints one document (`pose.runtime.run/1`) with them and with `via` (`runtime` or `standalone`), the `fallback_reason`, the typed `outcome` (`completed`; `error` when the command ran and ended but its output (over 2 MiB) is not sent back, with the command's exit code; or `unknown` for a deadline or a lost answer), the epoch and the memo hits. The runtime refuses, before the command starts, one it does not host and a caller whose environment digest (the `POSE_`, `HARNE8_`, `GIT_`, `CLAUDE` and `CODEX` variables, `XDG_STATE_HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, the locale, `HOME`, `NO_COLOR` and the user) differs from its own, so the caller runs standalone; a command that reached the runtime and whose answer did not come back whole, or whose `--timeout` passed, may have run and is never repeated standalone unasked: it exits 1 and says so. The runtime runs hosted commands as the user that started it, in its worktree, with the output of a non-terminal, in the admission lanes described below; a command still waiting for its slot when its `--timeout` passes is refused, not started, so the caller runs it standalone. A runtime also keeps, between commands, the output of the Git reads that are pure functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by that commit and the shallow state (256 MiB, least recently used first; not for a linked worktree): the second `review-check` of this repository took about 10 s against about 25 s standalone, with the same output. The runtime admits hosted commands in two lanes, each with its own slots and a bounded queue: `short` (`surface-check`, about 10 ms on this repository) and `heavy` (the other hosted commands; `roadmap-check <slug>` takes minutes here), 2 and 1 slots by default and queues of 16 and 4; `pose runtime start|serve` take `--short-slots`, `--heavy-slots`, `--short-queue`, `--heavy-queue` and `--memo-mib` (default 256). A short query is not queued behind a heavy check, and a command that finds every slot of its lane busy and the queue full is refused before it waits and runs standalone. `status` and the handshake carry, per lane, `<lane>_slots`, `_queue_limit`, `_queued`, `_running`, `_admitted`, `_refused`, `_wait_ms_total` and `_wait_ms_max` in `metrics`. A measurement of the runtime on a real repository, with its thresholds fixed before the numbers, is `POSE_RUNTIME_MEASURE=<file> go test ./internal/cli -run TestMeasureTheRuntimeOnTheRealRepository`; its report is under `.pose/reports/`.\\n\\n**Review rounds.** The rounds of a scope are its sealed bundles that a completed delegated review concluded on, in the order those runs ended; `pose review verify`, `pose delegate ls` and `pose insights` show the round. From round 2, the generated brief (template `review-brief/2`; runs briefed with `review-brief/1` still verify) carries a `Previous round` section built from the ledger: every open finding with the identifier it keeps across rounds (`R<round>-<id>`), its severity, disposition and location, the previous run and its draft digest. The reviewer's draft is the fenced code block whose info string is `pose-draft`: JSON with `schema` `pose.review.draft/v1`, `decision` and `findings` (`id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line`, `summary`), and from round 2 `prior_findings` (`id`, `status` fixed|partial|not-fixed|superseded, `evidence`) and `sweep`. A finding reported `partial` or `not-fixed` keeps its identifier in the next brief. A round-2-or-later draft that leaves an open finding unaccounted, calls one `fixed` without evidence or states no sweep is recorded with `draft_problems`, `wait` reports it as `invalid`, and it is never accepted as an approval. With `routing.rounds` `continue-thread` (the default) a round resumes the session the previous round ended with, through the agent's resume command; it opens a new session, recording why, when the agent or its vendor changes, the chain of drafts is broken, that session cannot be resumed, or the round reaches `routing.round_threshold` (5 by default), where `verify` also warns that the rounds are not converging. `cold-session` opens a new session every round. Each run records its `round` and `thread`; `pose insights` reports, per scope, findings per round, rounds to approval, and how prior findings were accounted for in continued and in cold rounds. A previous round whose record is not intact makes the brief refuse.\n\n**Delegated runs over MCP.** `pose_delegate_start` (review, adjudicate or smoke), `pose_delegate_status`, `pose_delegate_wait`, `pose_delegate_resume` and `pose_delegate_handoff` run `pose delegate … --json` in the project the server resolved for the call, so every plan, refusal and run is the CLI's: a start, resume or handoff is always detached and returns once the run has reserved its attempt (without `apply` it previews), a status never blocks, and a wait blocks at most its `timeout_seconds` (300 at most), returning `still-running` when the bound passes. Refusals carry the same `reason_code` as the CLI's error document (`rerun-without-reason`, `budget-exhausted`, `containment-incompatible`, `agent-unavailable`, `no-agent`), and results carry the normalized summary only: no transcript, prompt, session reference or environment value. `pose delegate resume` and `handoff` take `--detach` as the purposes do. A task is not started over MCP.\n\n### Keep delegated work proportional\n\nDelegate a bounded question or change with named paths, acceptance criteria,\nallowed access and a stopping condition. Give the worker relevant references\nand excerpts; keep full transcripts and command output in files. Use the native\n`task` purpose for implementation or investigation and the generated sealed brief\nfor governed review, adjudication or smoke conclusions.\n\n- Keep coordination and mechanical CLI steps with the primary execution. Do not\n  add a secondary coordinator merely to watch another worker or fill CLI forms.\n- Inspect `agents` and preview the route once before starting. Record the chosen\n  model, vendor, effort, access and attempt; preserve the independence required\n  by the sealed plan.\n- Use native `watch` or `wait` to follow a run. Report stage changes and findings;\n  do not spend agent turns repeatedly reading unchanged logs.\n- Resume or hand off the existing attempt when its state permits. Preserve failed\n  and quota-limited records and respect their budgets. Do not start a fresh run\n  to bypass a limit or invent a reset time the provider did not report.\n- Before a detached review of a new scope, ensure the sealed checkout contains\n  matching indexed attribution and structured validation for its source. Persist\n  missing derived inputs before final validation; uncommitted results and indexes\n  are unavailable in that checkout.\n- Check provider authentication and output/capture framing when configuring an\n  agent or after a setup failure. Availability and a routing preview do not prove\n  a successful model request or a verifiable conclusion.\n- Run focused regressions while changing code. Reuse current canonical evidence\n  when native coverage and provenance support it, and repeat checks invalidated\n  by a material change. Preserve required checks, controls and independent\n  judgments; a task's success is not review approval.\n- Reuse applicable corpus and fixtures. Add an extra experiment only for an\n  identified risk, contract or finding, with a bounded scope. Qualification of a\n  separate engine-wide pilot is not an automatic checklist for every spec.\n- Track machine time and CPU/RAM separately from model input/output and cached\n  tokens. Do not infer billed cost or unique context from cumulative token counts.\n\nUse these practices within the existing review plan and task workflow. They do\nnot create another mandatory rehearsal, report or approval layer. Keep the\ntechnical details of execution routing in the sections below.\n\n### Bound delegated adapter execution\n\nDeclare an optional `containment` object on each agent in `.pose/policy/delegation.json`. Use containment schema 1 and `trusted-process`. This backend runs a trusted local executable; a disposable worktree shares Git metadata and does not isolate filesystem, original repository, network, credential files, CPU or memory. Required guarantees that the host cannot enforce are refused before reserving an attempt or spawning a process. Linux process-group termination requires an observable process inventory and is checked after kill; descendants that escape the group remain outside its coverage. Windows/other ports do not qualify group containment from a cross-build.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"containment\": {\n    \"schema_version\": 1,\n    \"profile\": \"trusted-process\",\n    \"required\": [\"bounded-capture\", \"environment-allowlist\"],\n    \"environment\": [\"LANG\"],\n    \"credentials\": [\"REVIEWER_API_KEY\"],\n    \"stream_bytes\": 8388608,\n    \"output_bytes\": 65536,\n    \"retained_bytes\": 16777216,\n    \"retention_ttl_seconds\": 0\n  }\n}\n```\n\nKeep environment/credentials lists unique ignoring case (at most 128/32 names). Ambient token variables are excluded. Explicit profiles use a private disposable HOME; an omitted profile is disclosed as `legacy-trusted` and retains HOME compatibility. This is environment filtering, not protection against an executable reading same-user files. Authorize credential variable names explicitly; never put their values in policy, argv, brief or notes. Loader hooks and Git redirection variables cannot be allowlisted. Credential values and captured session references are redacted together in one bounded pass, longest match first; this is exact-value redaction, not general data-loss prevention or a guarantee against encoded secrets emitted by an untrusted adapter.\n\nUse a positive timeout from 1ms to 24h (omitted: 60m). Omit `max_runs_per_bundle` for a finite default of three; explicit values must be 1..100, including on legacy adapters. Explicit zero/null is invalid. Omitted stream/output/retained limits are 8MiB/64KiB/16MiB; explicit ranges are 1..64MiB, 1..128KiB and 1..128MiB. Require retained bytes to cover `stream_bytes + 6*output_bytes + 1048576` for JSON escaping and metadata. The retained budget covers the run record plus transcript per execution; independent bounded ledger/proof inventories still apply. It is not an aggregate storage quota, an adapter memory quota or an OS disk limit. Memory/CPU settings, positive TTL and unknown enforcement fields are refused by this backend.\n\nBound setup to two minutes and 64KiB of discarded diagnostic capture; disable ambient checkout hooks. Bound concurrent stdout/stderr together during capture and read `{output}` through a root descriptor opened before spawn, refusing links, special files, identity changes and oversized files. Notes must be regular non-symlink UTF-8 files up to 16KiB. Do not interpolate brief/notes into shell code. Truncated, unsafe or unredactable channels make the execution failed/incomplete; withhold their bytes because a cutoff can split a credential. No such draft governs review authority. Caller cancellation and CLI interrupt/termination signals stop the covered process set; pipe wait is bounded.\n\nInspect `safety` on the immutable run, CLI dispatch/verify/resume and the existing MCP review-bundle projection. It reports profile/platform, available/unavailable capabilities, limits, capture completeness, execution cause, runtime resource identifier and the B12 cleanup OperationResult. Cleanup verifies operation ownership and removes only the captured workspace and its exact Git registration through confined descriptors, never a global Git prune. Inventory caps are 100000 workspace entries/256MiB, 1024 registration entries/256MiB and 4096 runtime entries/128MiB, depth 128. OS filesystem calls remain subject to I/O latency; no hard syscall deadline is claimed. Failure retains uncertain resources and is separate from the domain draft. It returns an operational error, blocks authority/continuation and remains an error on idempotent replay, without another spawn. Inspect the recorded runtime identifier and registration rather than rerunning the adapter to compensate.\n\nRetain evidence without automatic expiry (`retention_ttl_seconds: 0`); newly written run/transcript files use mode 0600 and private sessions retain their existing 0700/0600 policy. Export retained redacted run/transcript, witness, attempt/continuation receipts and optional proof together, preserving inventory and digests; exclude private sessions and issuer keys. Raw-byte digests precede redaction; retained-byte digests, canonical run identity, witness and native signature follow. No retention operation removes rejection history or restores quota. Legacy records remain readable with no fabricated safety observations. No instance policy, sandbox backend or real vendor is activated by these examples.\n\n### Continue interrupted delegated reviews\n\nConfigure optional `limit`, `session` and `resume` fields on an agent in `.pose/policy/delegation.json`. Use RE2 patterns; session/reset patterns need exactly one capture group. Recognize limits by declared exit codes or output patterns in stdout/stderr or `{output}`. Parse reset captures as RFC3339 instants. The engine does not infer vendor vocabulary. Example fields on an otherwise configured agent:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"limit\": {\"exit_codes\": [75], \"output_patterns\": [\"LIMIT\"], \"reset_pattern\": \"reset: (\\\\S+)\"},\n  \"session\": {\"pattern\": \"session: (\\\\S+)\"},\n  \"resume\": {\"command\": [\"reviewer\", \"resume\", \"{session}\", \"--output\", \"{output}\"], \"max_resumes\": 3, \"max_wall_time\": \"60m\"}\n}\n```\n\nPass `{session}` as an entire argv element, never inside shell code. Captures from both output channels must agree. Session references are saved under `.pose/review-private/` (directory 0700/files 0600, self-ignored by Git); keep that runtime material private. Public records contain the session digest. The supervisor redacts the captured reference from both transcript and draft before publishing; ambiguous captures withhold the unredacted bytes. Losing private material makes a run not recoverable; no replacement session is invented.\n\nRun `pose delegate ls [--json]` to inspect interruptions, whether a partial draft exists, reset instants and remaining wait. `pose doctor` warns about interrupted attempt endpoints. Runs finish completed, failed or interrupted; legacy stale records remain readable. Undeclared interruptions are unknown and not resumable. An interrupted draft is partial evidence, never a rejection or a review.\n\nRun `pose delegate resume <run>` (`--dry-run` previews it; repeating it returns the recorded continuation) to continue with the same agent, captured session, brief digest, sealed commit and reconstructed checkout digest. Resume uses the original attempt and does not spend another attempt quota. The default maximum is three resumes and 60 minutes of accumulated wall-clock; configure `resume.max_resumes`/`max_wall_time` to change these bounds. Policy changes, changed inputs, lost or rewritten predecessors, terminal predecessors and spent budgets refuse. An immutable predecessor claim permits only one continuation; repeated requests return the recorded result or expose its pending reservation without launching another process. A lost host before a result is published remains explicit incomplete evidence; this is not automatic recovery without a captured interrupted record.\n\nUse `--wait-until-reset[=<duration>]` to wait once for a recorded reset, plus at most one second of jitter, then revalidate and resume. Default and maximum wait are 24 hours; a missing or more distant reset refuses. No vendor polling or automatic fallback occurs. To choose another agent after a pending usage limit, run it explicitly with `--agent <name>`; this creates a new attempt with `limit-of:<run>` and discloses both runs. Insights reports resumes separately from attempts and reruns; declared draft approval rates use completed unsuperseded drafts only.\n\n### Verify signed delegated run provenance\n\nOnce a scope is done, the approval a delegated run backed is verified as it was sealed: the proof's signature under an issuer that still holds its pin, the exact run record, transcript and bundle it binds, and that no later run superseded it. It is not re-verified against the repository as it is now, so a later change to a file the review sealed (the validation matrix, this manual) does not unseat it; revoking the issuer's pin, altering the record or reopening the scope does. An open scope keeps the live checks.\n\nDeclare the executor and implementation baseline before dispatch. A bundle that records its implementation identity is compared with that identity: `provenance.implementation` is read only for bundles sealed before bundles recorded it, and a dispatch warns when the policy still declares it for a later bundle. A run-backed different-actor attestation needs the sealed identity to name a known vendor and the sealing session's execution; seal from the implementing session, or with `--lead`, and read the seal's warnings. `verify` and the closeout disclose the identity's source and the independence the run achieved. Add `provenance` to the delegation policy and an `executor` agent principal to every agent. A schema 1 policy takes the same fields on its adapters, as below. The following identities are synthetic examples; record the real declared baseline rather than inferring it from a reviewer name:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"provenance\": {\n    \"trust\": \"executor-attested\",\n    \"implementation\": {\"principal\": \"agent:implementer\", \"execution\": \"implementation-run-1\", \"vendor\": \"vendor-a\", \"model\": \"model-a\"}\n  },\n  \"adapters\": {\n    \"reviewer\": {\"executor\": \"agent:reviewer\", \"command\": [\"reviewer\", \"--output\", \"{output}\"], \"vendor\": \"vendor-b\", \"model\": \"model-b\"}\n  }\n}\n```\n\nUse the existing native issuer and its attestation pin. Set protected `authority_project` and `authority_audience` in review policy; a declared project identity must agree. Keep keys outside the project. Dispatch captures origin/worktree, bundle/commit/scope/kind, configuration/argv/executable, identities, brief template/body/notes, planned stdin and actual pipe-write digests, raw and retained redacted output/transcript digests and completeness. A pipe write does not prove the vendor consumed the prompt. Session references and private keys stay outside the public proof.\n\nPreview `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <name> [--ttl <duration>]`, then add `--apply` to publish an immutable proof under `.pose/review-run-proofs/`. TTL is optional (no expiry when absent), positive when supplied and at most 365 days. Verify it with `pose review run-proof <run-id> <proof-id> [--json]`. The proof signs the captured executor declaration; it is not an independent vendor receipt or human confirmation. Failed or interrupted audit records disclose their state and `complete_review=false`. Unknown or ambiguous draft decisions also keep that flag false. A true flag describes completed delivery with one draft decision, not approval or complete typed-conclusion validation. A completed run with incomplete stdin or truncated output cannot establish a completed review. Legacy runs have no captured provenance and cannot be upgraded by signing today's configuration. `pose review run-proof` and the issuer lookups of the verdict readers ask the question the closeout asks: for a run whose scope is done, the proof as it was given (the record stands, the issuer still holds its pin; routine drift of the repository and the proof's expiry are not asked, because the expiry bounds when a proof may back a new attestation, not whether an attestation already given to a done scope stands); for an open scope, as the repository is now.\n\nAttach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `pose review attest` request. The native issuer derives authority principal/execution bindings from the run and signs the exact reference. Criteria, tools, evidence and all ordinary preflight checks still apply. A rejecting or ambiguous draft cannot support an approval; a run-backed attestation cannot predate its completed run or signed proof. Adjudication/smoke run proofs bind their kinds but cannot be used as review attestations. Automatic validation/application of complete typed conclusions belongs to the capability, not to run signing.\n\n**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/7` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/7` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n\n**Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\n\nOpt in to the verification bar with `delegated_review: true` in review policy only when preparing adoption. This live bar requires a signed run reference for agent different-actor review and refuses a prefix-only identity; it does not enable or add the future catalog capability. Non-adopting instances keep declared behavior. Under explicit executor-attested trust, normalized vendor OR model difference satisfies different-actor (vendor difference preferred); unknown baselines and the same vendor/model refuse. A machine run never satisfies mandatory-human. Catalog adoption, cutoffs/rollback and automatic conclusion application remain separate capability work.\n\nRead the same current assurance through verify, review-check, MCP and `pose state --attention`: `machine-attested` names the signed executor declaration, its source, run/proof and vendor/model comparisons. Current issuer pins, project/audience/time, sealed inputs, retained artifacts and original/continuation receipts are reverified. Revocation, missing receipts, rewritten data or stale inputs remove machine assurance. Signing does not reset quotas or erase earlier rejecting runs. The origin worktree is recorded with the run, not required of the reader: a proof verifies in any checkout of the project, so a run a CI job or the Conductor performed is verified like a local one; no universal containment is claimed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
